(window.SITE_PARTS=window.SITE_PARTS||{})["bill-rights"]={"plain":{"bills":{"rights":{"slug":"rights","title":"The Enforceable Rights Act","sections":[{"anchor":"sec-0-1","heading":"\u00a7 0-1. Short title","part":"SECTION 0 \u2014 SHORT TITLE, FINDINGS, DEFINITIONS","body":"This Act may be cited as the **Enforceable Rights Act**.","partHeads":[{"heading":"SECTION 0 \u2014 SHORT TITLE, FINDINGS, DEFINITIONS","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-0-2","heading":"\u00a7 0-2. Findings","part":"SECTION 0 \u2014 SHORT TITLE, FINDINGS, DEFINITIONS","body":"1. Congress enacted the Civil Rights Act of 1871 to provide a remedy in federal court against persons who, acting under color of State law, deprive others of rights secured by the Constitution. Congress imposed **no immunity defense** in the text it enacted.\n2. The defense now known as **qualified immunity** was not enacted by Congress. It requires a plaintiff to identify a prior decision holding materially identical conduct unlawful, with the consequence that **the first person subjected to a novel violation has no remedy**, and no decision establishing the right is ever rendered.\n3. Courts have discretion to resolve such cases on the immunity question without deciding whether a right was violated. The predictable result is that **rights are neither vindicated nor clarified**, and the body of law against which \"clearly established\" is measured does not grow.\n4. A damages remedy against **federal** officers, recognized judicially in 1971, has since been confined to substantially the facts of the three cases in which it was applied. **Persons injured by federal officers are, in the ordinary case, without any remedy at all.**\n5. A municipality is presently liable only where the injury was caused by an official policy or custom. A person injured by an employee acting within the scope of employment therefore has a remedy against a private employer that the same person lacks against a government employer, **for the same conduct**.\n6. Congress finds a **continuing and widespread pattern** of deprivations of rights secured by the Fourteenth Amendment by persons acting under color of State law, that existing remedies have proved inadequate to deter or redress them, and that the remedies provided in this Act are **congruent and proportional** to the injuries to be prevented and remedied.\n7. Where the announcement of an unlawful action accomplishes its object, relief obtained years later does not remedy the injury. **A remedy that arrives after the conduct has achieved its purpose is not a remedy**, and the cost of delay should fall on the party that initiated the action.\n8. Immunity doctrines were adopted to protect officials from the burdens of litigation. **Indemnification, not immunity, is the means by which that protection is in fact delivered** in the overwhelming majority of cases, and it is delivered without the costs that immunity imposes on the development of constitutional law."},{"anchor":"sec-0-3","heading":"\u00a7 0-3. Definitions","part":"SECTION 0 \u2014 SHORT TITLE, FINDINGS, DEFINITIONS","body":"**(a) Covered defendant.** A person who, under color of any statute, ordinance, regulation, custom, or usage of any State, territory, or the District of Columbia, or under color of Federal law or authority, subjects another to a deprivation of a right secured by the Constitution or laws of the United States.\n\n**(b) Governmental employer.** The United States, a State, a political subdivision of a State, or any agency or instrumentality of any of them.\n\n**(c) Protected activity.** Petitioning a court, filing or maintaining an action under this Act or any other law, testifying, providing evidence, reporting a violation, or assisting another in doing any of these things."},{"anchor":"sec-1-1","heading":"\u00a7 1-1. Qualified immunity abolished","part":"SECTION 1 \u2014 IMMUNITY","body":"**(a)** In any action to redress the deprivation of a right secured by the Constitution or laws of the United States, **it shall not be a defense** that:\n1. the right was not clearly established at the time of the conduct;\n2. no court had previously held materially similar conduct unlawful;\n3. the defendant acted in good faith, or reasonably believed the conduct lawful; or\n4. the defendant relied on a statute, regulation, policy, order, judicial opinion, or legal advice later held or conceded to be invalid.\n\n**(b) Abolition, not narrowing.** The defense described in subsection (a) is **abolished** and may not be reconstituted under another name, as an element of the plaintiff's case, as a heightened pleading requirement, or as a component of the merits of any constitutional claim.\n\n**(c) Reliance as mitigation.** Evidence described in subsection (a)(4) is inadmissible on liability and **admissible only in mitigation of punitive damages**.\n\n**(d) The constitutional question shall be reached.** In an action under this Act, a court shall determine whether the conduct alleged violated the Constitution or laws of the United States. **A court may not dispose of such a claim without making that determination** on the ground that any other defense is available \u2014 except where the claim is resolved on the pleadings for failure to allege facts, on the ground that the conduct did not occur, or on any ground unrelated to the lawfulness of the conduct.","partHeads":[{"heading":"SECTION 1 \u2014 IMMUNITY","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-1-2","heading":"\u00a7 1-2. Prosecutorial immunity \u2014 carve-out","part":"SECTION 1 \u2014 IMMUNITY","body":"**(a)** Absolute immunity shall not bar an action for damages against a prosecutor for:\n1. the **knowing** use of testimony or evidence the prosecutor knew to be false;\n2. the **knowing** suppression of evidence favorable to the accused and material to guilt or punishment;\n3. the fabrication of evidence; or\n4. the initiation or maintenance of a charge that the prosecutor knew was not supported by probable cause, **where the charge was initiated because of the person's exercise of a right protected by the First Amendment**.\n\n**(b) Scienter is an element.** Each paragraph of subsection (a) requires actual knowledge, which the plaintiff must plead with particularity and prove by clear and convincing evidence.\n\n**(c) Preserved.** Immunity is otherwise unaffected, including as to charging judgments, evaluations of evidence, and the conduct of trial."},{"anchor":"sec-1-2a","heading":"\u00a7 1-2A. Referral on a finding","part":"SECTION 1 \u2014 IMMUNITY","body":"**(a) Duty to refer.** Where a court of the United States **makes an express finding, on the record**, that a prosecutor engaged in conduct described in \u00a7 1-2(a) \u2014 including the knowledge that \u00a7 1-2(b) requires \u2014 **the court shall transmit the finding** to each authority licensing the prosecutor to practice law and to the office responsible for professional responsibility within the prosecutor's employing agency. **The duty is ministerial.**\n\n**(b) Findings in State courts.** Where a court of a State makes such a finding, **any party may transmit it** to the authorities described in subsection (a). This Section imposes no duty on any State court.\n\n**(c) Notice and an opportunity to be heard.** Before transmitting a finding under subsection (a), the court shall **give the prosecutor notice and an opportunity to be heard** on whether the conduct occurred, unless the prosecutor was a party and had that opportunity.\n\n**(d) What is transmitted.** The transmission contains **the finding and the record supporting it**, and states no conclusion as to what discipline, if any, is warranted.\n\n**(d-1) What is not a finding.** A ruling that assumes conduct **arguendo**, a disposition on an alternative ground, a reversal or grant of relief that does not rest on a finding of the knowledge \u00a7 1-2(b) requires, and a statement in dicta, **are not findings for purposes of subsection (a).**\n\n**(e) The referral decides nothing.** The licensing authority and the employing agency **determine independently** what action to take. Nothing in this Section directs, requires, or bears upon that determination, and **no consequence under this Section attaches to the prosecutor by force of the referral itself.**\n\n**(f) No immunity as to the referral.** No immunity, privilege, or defense bars a transmission under this Section, which is a report of what a court found and not a claim against any person.\n\n**(g) Publication.** Each employing agency shall publish annually **the number of findings transmitted to it under this Section, the number resulting in an investigation, and the number resulting in discipline of any kind**, without identifying any person.\n\n**(h) Independent of \u00a7 1-2.** This Section operates **whether or not any action for damages has been brought or could be brought**, and a finding under it is not a judgment, does not establish liability, and does not bear on any element of a claim under \u00a7 1-2. **Neither Section limits the other.**\n\n**(i) Severability.** This Section is severable from \u00a7 1-2 and from every other provision. **If \u00a7 1-2 is held invalid, this Section remains in force**, and if subsection (a) is held invalid, subsection (b) is unaffected."},{"anchor":"sec-1-3","heading":"\u00a7 1-3. Judicial and legislative immunity preserved","part":"SECTION 1 \u2014 IMMUNITY","body":"Nothing in this Act limits the immunity of a judge for judicial acts, or of a legislator for legislative acts."},{"anchor":"sec-2-1","heading":"\u00a7 2-1. Action against federal officers","part":"SECTION 2 \u2014 THE FEDERAL CAUSE OF ACTION","body":"**(a) Cause of action.** Any person injured by the deprivation, under color of Federal law or authority, of a right secured by the Constitution may bring a civil action for damages and equitable relief against the officer or employee responsible, in the district court of the United States.\n\n**(b) No special-factors limitation.** The action provided by this Section is **statutory**. A court may not decline to recognize or entertain it on the ground that special factors counsel hesitation, that an alternative remedial structure exists, that the context is new, or that Congress is better suited to weigh the costs and benefits \u2014 Congress having weighed them in enacting this Section.\n\n**(b-1) Grounds foreclosed by name.** The grounds foreclosed by subsection (b) include that the defendant was engaged in immigration enforcement, border security, or the protection of the national security; that the defendant was employed by a person other than the United States; and that the conduct occurred in or near a facility in which persons are held in custody. **Congress has considered each and provides this action notwithstanding.**\n\n**(c) Alternative remedies.** The availability of an administrative remedy, a grievance procedure, a claim under chapter 171 of title 28, **or a remedy under the law of any State \u2014 including any action for negligence, assault, battery, wrongful death, or medical malpractice, and whether against an officer, an employee, or a private person \u2014 does not displace this action.** That a State provides a remedy is **not a reason to withhold the remedy provided here**, and no court may treat the adequacy, availability, or unavailability of a State remedy as bearing on whether this action lies. Amounts recovered elsewhere for the same injury are set off.\n\n**(c-1) The judgment bar of chapter 171.** Section 2676 of title 28 **shall not apply** to a claim under this Act. A judgment in an action under section 1346(b) of title 28 is not a bar to a claim under this Act, and does not bar such a claim **whether it is entered before, at the same time as, or after** the claim under this Act, and without regard to whether the two claims arise from the same subject matter. Subsection (c) addresses the **availability** of a remedy under that chapter; this subsection addresses **a judgment entered in it**, and neither limits the other.\n\n**(c-2) The exclusive-remedy provision of chapter 171.** A claim under this Act is a civil action described in **section 2679(b)(2)(A) of title 28**, being brought for a violation of the Constitution, and is also one described in **section 2679(b)(2)(B)**, being brought under a statute of the United States under which such an action against an individual is authorized. **Section 2679(b)(1) does not apply to a claim under this Act.** The statement in subsection (b) that the action provided by this Section is statutory is made to foreclose the special-factors inquiry, and **does not remove the action from section 2679(b)(2)(A) or place it outside the Constitution's protection.**\n\n**(c-3) Enumeration is a determination.** The provisions of chapter 171 of title 28 named in subsections (c-1) and (c-2) are displaced as to a claim under this Act. **The omission from this Section of any other limitation appearing in that chapter is a determination by Congress that the limitation does not apply to a claim under this Act**, and is not a gap to be filled by reference to that chapter.\n\n**(d) Territorial scope; physical presence controls.**\n1. This Section applies to conduct occurring within the United States. **The Act does not create a cause of action for acts of the armed forces in the course of armed conflict abroad.**\n1-A. **Either end suffices.** Conduct occurs within the United States where the covered defendant **acted from within it**, or where the person against whom the conduct was directed was **physically present within it**, and it is immaterial that the other end of the conduct, or the injury, was elsewhere. **This paragraph does not narrow the exclusion stated in paragraph (1)**, which applies to an act of the armed forces in the course of armed conflict abroad without regard to where any person acted.\n2. A person **physically present within the United States** is within the United States for purposes of this Act, **without regard to** whether the person has been admitted, has effected an entry, is treated by any other provision of law as an applicant for admission or as not having entered, is paroled, is in expedited or any other removal proceeding, or is held at or near a port of entry. **No legal fiction as to a person's location or entry governs the application of this Act.**\n3. **Custody abroad.** A claim for the deprivation of a right created by this Act, arising within custody described in \u00a7 8-1(d), may be brought under this Section notwithstanding the first sentence of paragraph (1). **This paragraph does not narrow that paragraph's second sentence**, and creates no claim for an act of the armed forces in the course of armed conflict.","partHeads":[{"heading":"SECTION 2 \u2014 THE FEDERAL CAUSE OF ACTION","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-2-2","heading":"\u00a7 2-2. Scope of employment","part":"SECTION 2 \u2014 THE FEDERAL CAUSE OF ACTION","body":"The United States shall be substituted as defendant for a claim under \u00a7 2-1 only where it accepts liability without reservation and waives every defense going to the availability of the remedy."},{"anchor":"sec-3-1","heading":"\u00a7 3-1. Employer liability","part":"SECTION 3 \u2014 GOVERNMENTAL LIABILITY","body":"**(a)** A governmental employer is liable for the acts of its officers and employees committed **within the scope of employment**, to the same extent as a private employer under the law of the State in which the conduct occurred.\n\n**(b) No policy-or-custom requirement.** It is **not** an element of a claim against a governmental employer that the deprivation was caused by an official policy, custom, practice, ratification, or deliberate indifference, or that the employer was the moving force behind it.\n\n**(c) Supervisors.** A supervisor is liable for a subordinate's deprivation where the supervisor directed it, knew of it and failed to act, or was deliberately indifferent to a known risk of it.\n\n**(d) Contractors.** A person who performs a function of a governmental employer under a contract, agreement, or arrangement with that employer **is liable under this Section to the same extent as the governmental employer would be**, and subsection (b) applies to that person. It is **not a defense** that the person is not a governmental employer, is organized for profit, or performs the function for compensation, and **no requirement of policy, custom, practice, ratification, or deliberate indifference applies** to a claim against such a person.","partHeads":[{"heading":"SECTION 3 \u2014 GOVERNMENTAL LIABILITY","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-3-2","heading":"\u00a7 3-2. State sovereign immunity","part":"SECTION 3 \u2014 GOVERNMENTAL LIABILITY","body":"**(a) Abrogation.** Congress, acting under section 5 of the Fourteenth Amendment and on the findings in \u00a7 0-2(6), abrogates the immunity of the States from suit for claims under this Act arising from deprivations of rights secured by the Fourteenth Amendment.\n\n**(b) Limited.** This Section reaches only such claims, and provides only compensatory damages and equitable relief against a State. **Punitive damages are not available against a State.**\n\n**(c) Severability \u2014 and what survives.** If subsection (a) is held invalid in whole or in part, **every other provision of this Act continues in effect**, including claims against officers in their individual capacities, claims for prospective relief against State officers, and claims against political subdivisions \u2014 **which possess no immunity under the Eleventh Amendment** and against which \u00a7 3-1 operates without regard to this Section."},{"anchor":"sec-4-1","heading":"\u00a7 4-1. Transparency","part":"SECTION 4 \u2014 INDEMNIFICATION","body":"A governmental employer that indemnifies or defends a covered defendant shall publish annually the number of claims resolved, the total paid in judgments and settlements, and the number of covered defendants indemnified more than once in the preceding five years. **Individual identities need not be published.**","partHeads":[{"heading":"SECTION 4 \u2014 INDEMNIFICATION","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-4-2","heading":"\u00a7 4-2. Limits","part":"SECTION 4 \u2014 INDEMNIFICATION","body":"A governmental employer may not indemnify a covered defendant for **punitive damages**, or for conduct the trier of fact found to have been undertaken with knowledge of its unlawfulness. Nothing in this Section requires or prohibits indemnification in any other case."},{"anchor":"sec-4-3","heading":"\u00a7 4-3. Purpose","part":"SECTION 4 \u2014 INDEMNIFICATION","body":"The purpose of this Section is to make the fiscal consequence of unlawful conduct visible to the officials who set budgets and supervise personnel. **It does not shift the cost to individual defendants**, whose protection Congress finds is properly delivered through indemnification rather than immunity."},{"anchor":"sec-5-1","heading":"\u00a7 5-1. Prison Litigation Reform Act","part":"SECTION 5 \u2014 REMOVING BARRIERS TO ADJUDICATION","body":"**(a) Physical injury.** Section 1997e(e) of title 42 **shall not apply** to a claim under this Act. **No showing of physical injury is a prerequisite to any remedy provided by this Act**, including compensatory damages for mental or emotional injury.\n\n**(b) Exhaustion.** Section 1997e(a) of title 42 **shall not apply** to a claim under this Act. The rule stated in \u00a7 7-3(b) applies notwithstanding the reference in section 1997e(a) to \"any other Federal law.\"\n\n**(c) Attorney's fees.** Section 1997e(d) of title 42 **shall not apply** to a claim under this Act. Fees on such a claim are governed by \u00a7 6-3, and **no portion of a judgment awarded to the plaintiff shall be applied to satisfy an award of fees.**\n\n**(d) Prior dismissals.** Section 1915(g) of title 28 **shall not apply** to a claim under this Act. **Section 1915(e)(2) of title 28 is unaffected**, and a court retains its authority to dismiss a claim that is frivolous, malicious, or fails to state a claim upon which relief may be granted.\n\n**(e) Prospective relief.** As to a claim under this Act \u2014\n1. **subsections (b) and (e) of section 3626 of title 18** \u2014 termination of prospective relief, and the automatic stay of prospective relief upon a motion to terminate \u2014 **shall not apply**;\n2. prospective relief **shall not terminate by reason of the passage of time**, and shall be terminated or modified only upon a finding that the violation it remedies **has been remedied and cannot reasonably be expected to recur**, on which **the party seeking termination bears the burden**; and\n3. **subsection (a) of section 3626 is unaffected.** Prospective relief remains subject to the requirements that it extend no further than necessary to correct the violation, be narrowly drawn, and be the least intrusive means of correcting it, and to the procedures governing prisoner release orders.\n\n**(f) Construction.** Each rule stated in this Section applies **notwithstanding any provision of the Prison Litigation Reform Act of 1995 or of any amendment made by it.** The enumeration of the provisions named in this Section is a determination by Congress that **each named provision is displaced as to a claim under this Act.** The omission of any other provision of Federal law from this Section does not imply that it applies to such a claim, where another provision of this Act displaces it.","partHeads":[{"heading":"SECTION 5 \u2014 REMOVING BARRIERS TO ADJUDICATION","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-5-2","heading":"\u00a7 5-2. Members of the armed forces","part":"SECTION 5 \u2014 REMOVING BARRIERS TO ADJUDICATION","body":"A member of the armed forces may bring a claim under this Act for a deprivation **not arising out of combat, combat training, or operations in a zone of active hostilities.**"},{"anchor":"sec-5-3","heading":"\u00a7 5-3. Relation to confinement","part":"SECTION 5 \u2014 REMOVING BARRIERS TO ADJUDICATION","body":"A claim under this Act is barred by the validity of a conviction or sentence only where **success on the claim would necessarily** invalidate it. A claim challenging the conditions, duration, or lawfulness of the conduct of officers, which would not necessarily do so, may proceed."},{"anchor":"sec-5-4","heading":"\u00a7 5-4. Statutes of limitation","part":"SECTION 5 \u2014 REMOVING BARRIERS TO ADJUDICATION","body":"A claim under this Act may be brought within **four years** of the date the plaintiff knew or reasonably should have known of the injury and its cause."},{"anchor":"sec-5-5","heading":"\u00a7 5-5. State secrets","part":"SECTION 5 \u2014 REMOVING BARRIERS TO ADJUDICATION","body":"**(a) How the privilege is asserted.** The privilege against disclosure of state secrets may be asserted only by **the head of the department or agency** with control over the matter, **personally**, after **actual personal consideration**, in a writing filed with the court that identifies **the specific items** as to which it is asserted and the harm claimed for each.\n\n**(b) In camera review is mandatory.** The court **shall examine the material in camera.** An assertion under subsection (a) **does not establish the privilege**, and the privilege is established only as to items the court determines, item by item, would disclose a state secret.\n\n**(c) No dismissal on the assertion.** An action may not be dismissed, no claim or defense stricken, and no judgment entered, **on the basis of an assertion of the privilege.** Dismissal is available only after the court has completed the review required by subsection (b) and has considered every alternative under subsection (d).\n\n**(d) Alternatives considered first.** Before any dismissal, the court shall consider **an unclassified summary, a stipulation of the facts the item would establish, redaction, a protective order, disclosure to counsel holding an appropriate clearance, and the substitution of an admission**, and shall state on the record why each considered alternative is insufficient.\n\n**(e) Effect of a valid assertion.** Where the privilege is established as to an item, **the item is excluded from evidence** and the action proceeds without it so far as it can.\n\n**(f) The cost falls on the party asserting.** Where an item as to which the privilege is established is **essential to a claim or defense of a party opposing the United States**, the court may **resolve the issue to which the item relates against the United States** rather than dismiss the action. A party may not withhold evidence and rely on its absence.\n\n**(g) Not a pleading rule.** The privilege is **evidentiary.** It is not a bar to the commencement of an action, is not a ground for dismissal on the pleadings, and does not deprive any court of jurisdiction. **This subsection does not affect the separate rule that an action may not be maintained where the existence of the relationship or undertaking on which the action itself depends is a state secret**, which is narrow and is not enlarged by this Section.\n\n**(h) Interlocutory review.** An order determining the privilege is appealable by either party before final judgment, and **the material shall be transmitted to the reviewing court under seal.**"},{"anchor":"sec-5-6","heading":"\u00a7 5-6. Federal habeas corpus","part":"SECTION 5 \u2014 REMOVING BARRIERS TO ADJUDICATION","body":"**(a) Failure attributable to counsel is not the applicant's failure.** For purposes of section 2254(e)(2) of title 28, **an applicant has not \"failed to develop\" the factual basis of a claim where the failure is attributable to counsel in a State collateral proceeding, or to the absence of counsel in such a proceeding.** A person is not charged with the omissions of a lawyer the State was not required to provide.\n\n**(b) The innocence gate does not apply to claims about the proceeding itself.** Subparagraph (B) of section 2254(e)(2) **shall not apply** to a claim that counsel was constitutionally ineffective, that evidence favorable to the accused was suppressed, or that the tribunal was not impartial.\n\n**(c) Clearly established law.** For purposes of section 2254(d)(1) of title 28, **\"clearly established Federal law\" includes a proposition on which the courts of appeals are in agreement**, in addition to a holding of the Supreme Court.\n\n**(c-1) What subsection (c) does not do.** It does not make the decision of any court of appeals binding on any State court, does not alter the law a State court applies, and **does not permit relief on a proposition as to which the courts of appeals are divided.** Where they are divided, section 2254(d)(1) applies as written.\n\n**(d) Limitation and tolling.** The period under section 2244(d) of title 28 is **tolled** during any period in which the applicant was, without fault, unable to file \u2014 including a period of incompetency found by any court, a period during which the applicant was prevented from filing by an act or omission of the State, and a period during which the factual predicate was concealed by the State. **The applicant bears the burden of establishing the period and its cause**, and a period is tolled only for so long as the cause persisted. **Equitable tolling is available**, and section 2244(d) does not displace it.\n\n**(e) Preservation of jurisdiction.** Where a court has jurisdiction over an application under chapter 153 of title 28, and an act is imminent that would defeat the court's ability to grant relief, **the court may issue such order as is necessary to preserve its jurisdiction and the availability of relief**, under section 1651 of that title, before determining the merits. **An order under this subsection preserves the status quo and grants no relief on the application**, and it neither requires nor forecloses any determination on the merits. **An order that arrives after the act it would have prevented is not a remedy**, and the imminence of such an act is itself a ground for immediate relief under this subsection.\n\n**(f) What this Section does not do.** This Section states **no new ground for the writ**, alters no standard of proof on the merits, does not disturb the exhaustion requirement of section 2254(b), and **determines no question whether the Suspension Clause requires anything.** It removes conditions on being heard; it decides nothing about the outcome.\n\n**(g) Severability.** Each subsection is severable. **If subsection (c) is held invalid, subsections (a), (b), (d) and (e) remain in force** \u2014 and subsection (e) is severable from every other provision of this Act."},{"anchor":"sec-6-1","heading":"\u00a7 6-1. Retaliation prohibited","part":"SECTION 6 \u2014 PROTECTION OF PRIVATE ENFORCEMENT","body":"No person acting under color of Federal or State law shall take or threaten any adverse action against a person because of protected activity. A person subjected to such action has a claim under \u00a7 2-1 or under section 1983 of title 42, as applicable.","partHeads":[{"heading":"SECTION 6 \u2014 PROTECTION OF PRIVATE ENFORCEMENT","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-6-2","heading":"\u00a7 6-2. No compelled waiver","part":"SECTION 6 \u2014 PROTECTION OF PRIVATE ENFORCEMENT","body":"**(a) Unenforceable.** An agreement to waive the right to bring a claim under this Act, to arbitrate such a claim, or to submit it to a forum other than a court of the United States or of a State, **made before the conduct giving rise to the claim occurred**, is **unenforceable as to that claim** \u2014 whenever the agreement was made, and notwithstanding any other provision of law, including chapter 1 of title 9, United States Code.\n\n**(b) A court decides.** Whether this Section renders an agreement unenforceable as to a claim is **determined by a court**, notwithstanding any term delegating that determination to an arbitrator or incorporating rules that would so delegate. **The authority of an arbitrator over such a claim is conditioned on an agreement enforceable under this Section.**\n\n**(c) Settlement preserved.** This Section does not affect an agreement settling an existing claim, made after the conduct giving rise to it occurred **and identifying the claim it settles.** An instrument that identifies no claim settles none."},{"anchor":"sec-6-3","heading":"\u00a7 6-3. Fees","part":"SECTION 6 \u2014 PROTECTION OF PRIVATE ENFORCEMENT","body":"A prevailing plaintiff **shall** be awarded reasonable attorney's fees and costs. A prevailing defendant may be awarded fees only where the action was frivolous, unreasonable, or without foundation. **Fees shall not be reduced in proportion to the damages recovered**, and a settlement conditioned on waiver of fees is unenforceable as to the fee award. **No cap, ratio, or hourly-rate limitation stated in section 1997e(d) of title 42 applies to an award under this Section**, as provided in \u00a7 5-1(c)."},{"anchor":"sec-6-4","heading":"\u00a7 6-4. Interim relief","part":"SECTION 6 \u2014 PROTECTION OF PRIVATE ENFORCEMENT","body":"Where a plaintiff shows a likelihood of success on a claim that an action was taken because of protected activity, **the loss of the exercise of the right constitutes irreparable injury**, and the court shall consider that the passage of time during which the action remains in effect may itself accomplish the object of the action."},{"anchor":"sec-7-1","heading":"\u00a7 7-1. Defenses and immunities are exclusive","part":"SECTION 7 \u2014 CONSTRUCTION, DEFENSES, AND PROCEDURE","body":"**(a) Exclusive.** The defenses, immunities, privileges, exceptions, and limitations on liability available in an action under this Act are **those and only those expressly stated in this Act.**\n\n**(b) No common-law supplement.** The common law of the United States or of any State supplies no defense, immunity, privilege, or limitation on liability in an action under this Act. **No defense is to be inferred from the omission of any subject from this Act.** The absence of a provision addressing a defense or immunity recognized at common law is a determination by Congress that it is **not available**, and not an omission from which its availability may be inferred.\n\n**(c) No reconstitution.** A defense or immunity abolished by, or omitted from, this Act may not be reintroduced as an element of the plaintiff's case, a pleading requirement, a standard of proof, a component of the merits of a constitutional claim, a limitation on remedy, or a prudential limitation on jurisdiction.\n\n**(d) Enacted against a known background.** Congress enacts this Act with knowledge of the defenses and immunities that courts have applied to actions under section 1983 of title 42 and to actions against Federal officers. **The omission of any such defense from this Act is deliberate.**","partHeads":[{"heading":"SECTION 7 \u2014 CONSTRUCTION, DEFENSES, AND PROCEDURE","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-7-2","heading":"\u00a7 7-2. Construction","part":"SECTION 7 \u2014 CONSTRUCTION, DEFENSES, AND PROCEDURE","body":"**(a) Remedial.** This Act is remedial and shall be construed to give effect to its remedial purpose.\n\n**(b) Canons expressly addressed.** Congress has considered and **expressly displaces**, as to this Act: the presumption against abrogation of State sovereign immunity, to the extent stated in \u00a7 3-2; the presumption against the creation of causes of action, as to \u00a7\u00a7 2-1 and 6-1; and any presumption that a remedial scheme enacted elsewhere displaces a remedy provided here. **No such presumption applies to any provision of this Act.**\n\n**(c) Statutory, not implied.** Each cause of action created by this Act is created **by this Act**. No court may decline to recognize or entertain it on the ground that special factors counsel hesitation, that an alternative remedial structure exists, that the context is new, or that Congress is better positioned to weigh the consequences.\n\n**(d) Causation.** Liability under this Act requires that the conduct be a **but-for cause** of the deprivation. **No heightened causal standard applies**, and no requirement of proximate cause, moving force, affirmative link, or deliberate indifference applies except where this Act expressly so provides."},{"anchor":"sec-7-3","heading":"\u00a7 7-3. Procedure","part":"SECTION 7 \u2014 CONSTRUCTION, DEFENSES, AND PROCEDURE","body":"**(a) Pleading.** A complaint that alleges the conduct, the right deprived, and the defendant's connection to the deprivation satisfies the pleading requirements for a claim under this Act. **No heightened pleading requirement applies.** Knowledge, intent, motive, and reason may be alleged generally, and may be proved by circumstantial evidence, including evidence of departure from an agency's ordinary practice.\n\n**(b) No exhaustion; no channeling.** No administrative remedy, grievance procedure, or internal review is a prerequisite to an action under this Act, **including the requirement stated in section 1997e(a) of title 42**, which is displaced by \u00a7 5-1(b). **No statute establishing a remedial scheme shall be construed to channel, preclude, limit, or displace a claim under this Act unless that statute so provides by express reference to this Act.** The naming of section 1997e(a) in this subsection and in \u00a7 5-1 does not imply that any other exhaustion requirement survives this subsection.\n\n**(c) Mootness.** The cessation, suspension, expiration, or withdrawal of a challenged practice does not render an action under this Act moot where the defendant remains free to resume it. **A defendant asserting mootness bears the burden of establishing that the practice cannot reasonably be expected to recur**, and the voluntary cessation of a practice after the commencement of an action does not satisfy that burden.\n\n**(d) Discovery.** Where an element of a claim concerns the defendant's knowledge, reason, or practice, and the relevant records are within the defendant's control, **a claim may not be dismissed before the plaintiff has had a reasonable opportunity to obtain them.**"},{"anchor":"sec-7-4","heading":"\u00a7 7-4. Relief pending review","part":"SECTION 7 \u2014 CONSTRUCTION, DEFENSES, AND PROCEDURE","body":"**(a) Findings required.** A court granting or denying a stay or injunction pending review in an action under this Act shall **state its findings** as to each factor it applies, and the **duration** of any relief granted.\n\n**(b) Administrative stays.** An order entered **without those findings**, for the purpose of preserving the court's ability to rule, \u2014\n1. shall **state that purpose and its duration**;\n2. **expires after 14 days** unless extended by an order making the findings required by subsection (a); and\n3. **may not grant, in substance, the relief sought** on the application it preserves.\n\n**(c) Irreparable injury; predicate facts.** For purposes of any application under this Section, **the following constitute irreparable injury** \u2014\n1. removal of a person from the United States, or transfer of a person beyond the jurisdiction of the court, where the relief sought would be defeated by it;\n2. the **destruction, alteration, or loss** of evidence or records;\n3. the disbursement, obligation, or transfer of funds that could not be recovered; and\n4. the passage of time where **the action complained of would accomplish its object** before review could be completed.\n\n**(d) The applicant's showing.** Where the United States or an officer of the United States applies for a stay of an order, the application shall **identify the specific harm compliance would cause during the period of review**, and why that harm could not be avoided by narrowing the order.\n\n**(e) No presumption from the identity of a party.** **No party is entitled to any presumption** in its favor on an application under this Section by reason of being the United States, an officer of the United States, or a governmental employer.\n\n**(f) Nothing here directs a decision.** This Section **prescribes no outcome on any application.** It requires that findings be made and stated, fixes the duration of an order made without them, and identifies facts that constitute irreparable injury. **Whether relief should issue, and on what terms, remains for the court.**"},{"anchor":"sec-8-1","heading":"\u00a7 8-1. Application","part":"SECTION 8 \u2014 CONDITIONS OF CONFINEMENT","body":"**(a) Persons covered.** This Section applies to any person held in the custody of a covered defendant, **without regard to the authority under which the person is held** \u2014 including custody pursuant to conviction, sentence, pretrial detention, arrest, material-witness detention, civil commitment, immigration detention, and detention pending removal or transfer.\n\n**(b) Custodian.** A **custodian** is a covered defendant who holds a person in custody, operates or supervises a facility in which persons are held, or is responsible for the conditions in which they are held.\n\n**(c) Facilities operated under contract.** A person who holds another in custody **under a contract, agreement, or arrangement with a governmental employer acts under color of the law or authority of that employer** for purposes of this Act. Neither the private character of the custodian, nor the fact that the custody is performed for compensation, nor the terms of the contract, affects the application of this Section.\n\n**(d) Custody abroad.**\n\n1. **Application.** This Section, and Sections 9, 11, and 12 as they apply to conduct within custody, apply to custody operated by the United States, or by a person under a contract, agreement, or arrangement with the United States, **wherever the custody is carried out**, except within an area of active hostilities. Congress legislates here for **the conduct of its own officers, employees, and contractors**, extending to their custody abroad the principle it enacted at section 1003 of the Detainee Treatment Act of 2005 (42 U.S.C. \u00a7 2000dd) \u2014 which prohibits cruel, inhuman, or degrading treatment **without regard to nationality or physical location**, and to which this Act supplies the standards, records, and remedies that section lacks. **Section 10 is not extended by this subsection, and its omission is a determination of Congress.**\n\n2. **Area of active hostilities.** An **area of active hostilities** is a geographic area in which the armed forces of the United States are engaged in hostilities at the time of the conduct. **A designation, or the absence of one, by any officer neither establishes nor forecloses that fact.**\n\n3. **Remedies.** \u00a7 2-1(d)(3) provides the claim for the deprivation of a right created by this Act arising within custody described in paragraph (1).\n\n4. **No duty on any foreign sovereign.** This subsection imposes no duty on any foreign government. Where custody is carried out under an arrangement with a foreign government, the duties of this Act **condition the authority of the United States to enter into, renew, pay under, or transfer a person pursuant to the arrangement**, as \u00a7 8-7 provides, and bind the United States side alone. Nothing in this subsection displaces section 2242 of the Foreign Affairs Reform and Restructuring Act of 1998 (8 U.S.C. \u00a7 1231 note), which continues to govern transfers.\n\n5. **Severability by application.** If this subsection is held invalid, every application of this Act to custody within the United States is unaffected; and if its application to any place or class of custody is held invalid, every other application continues in force.","partHeads":[{"heading":"SECTION 8 \u2014 CONDITIONS OF CONFINEMENT","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-8-2","heading":"\u00a7 8-2. Standard of care","part":"SECTION 8 \u2014 CONDITIONS OF CONFINEMENT","body":"**(a) Duty.** A custodian shall not subject a person in custody to, or maintain, a condition of confinement posing a **substantial risk of serious harm** to that person.\n\n**(b) The standard is objective.** A custodian departs from subsection (a) where the custodian knew of the condition **or where a reasonable custodian in the same circumstances would have known of it**. It is **not an element** of a claim under this Section that the custodian actually perceived the risk, actually drew the inference that harm would follow, or subjectively disregarded it.\n\n**(c) What establishes knowledge.** Knowledge or constructive knowledge may be established by any evidence, including a grievance or request submitted by any person, a request for medical or mental-health care, a finding by any inspecting or accrediting body, a prior incident of substantially the same kind at the same facility, an order or judgment in another action, the custodian's own policies, and the ordinary and apparent characteristics of the facility.\n\n**(d) Duration and repetition.** A condition that would not pose a substantial risk of serious harm if brief may pose one if prolonged or repeated, and the trier of fact shall consider its duration and frequency."},{"anchor":"sec-8-2a","heading":"\u00a7 8-2A. Confinement is not additional punishment","part":"SECTION 8 \u2014 CONDITIONS OF CONFINEMENT","body":"**(a) The duty.** A custodian shall not impose or maintain a condition of confinement **for the purpose of punishment.** A condition is imposed for the purpose of punishment where no legitimate institutional objective may rationally be assigned to it, or where it is excessive in relation to the objective it is said to serve.\n\n**(b) The sentence is the punishment.** Where a person is held pursuant to a conviction, **the deprivation of liberty ordered by the court is the punishment authorized by law**, and a condition of confinement is not an additional punishment. A custodian may confine, and may maintain order, security and discipline; **it may not punish beyond the sentence.**\n\n**(c) Persons not held pursuant to conviction.** Where a person is held otherwise than pursuant to a conviction \u2014 including pretrial detention, arrest, material-witness detention, civil commitment, immigration detention, and detention pending removal or transfer \u2014 **no condition may be imposed for the purpose of punishment at all**, there being no sentence to serve. Detention pending removal is not punishment, and shall not be administered as though it were.\n\n**(d) Legitimate institutional objectives.** The following are legitimate institutional objectives for purposes of subsection (a): the security of the facility; the safety of persons in custody, staff, and visitors; the orderly operation of the facility; the health of persons in custody; classification and housing assignment; and ensuring appearance in a proceeding. **A sanction imposed for a violation of published institutional rules serves a legitimate objective** where it is imposed after the procedures the custodian's own published rules provide, is recorded with the rule violated and the facts found, and is proportionate in severity and duration to the violation. **\u00a7 8-4 continues to govern isolation** without regard to this subsection.\n\n**(d-1) The objective is recorded, not reconstructed.** Where a condition is imposed on an identified person and is expected to continue beyond the period set in the schedule to this Act, the custodian shall record the objective relied upon and the facts supporting it, before the period expires. **An existing classification, housing, disciplinary, or medical record stating the objective satisfies this subsection**, and no separate document is required. **An objective not recorded may not be asserted afterward** to justify the condition, and a condition for which no objective was recorded is one to which no legitimate institutional objective has been assigned for purposes of subsection (a). This subsection applies to a condition imposed on an identified person, and not to a condition of the facility generally.\n\n**(e) Purpose is determined objectively.** Whether a condition is imposed for the purpose of punishment is determined from **the condition itself, the objective the custodian states for it, the relationship between the two, and its duration and severity.** No showing of an intent to punish is required, and **it is not a defense that no officer intended to punish.** An expressed intent to punish is sufficient but never necessary.\n\n**(f) Threshold.** A condition causing only transient inconvenience, and that a reasonable custodian would not expect to affect a person's health, safety, ability to communicate with counsel or family, or access to food, water, sanitation, sleep, medical care, or religious observance, does not violate this Section. **The absence of physical injury is not a threshold**, and \u00a7 8-5's standard applies to \u00a7 8-2 and not to this Section.\n\n**(g) Relation to \u00a7 8-2.** This Section states a duty **separate from and additional to** \u00a7 8-2. A condition may violate this Section without posing a substantial risk of serious harm, and may violate \u00a7 8-2 though no punitive purpose is assignable to it. **Neither Section limits the other**, and a claim may be brought under either or both.\n\n**(h) A statutory duty.** The duty stated in this Section is created by this Act. **Its existence and content do not depend on the construction any court has given to the Eighth Amendment, to the Due Process Clause of the Fifth or Fourteenth Amendment, or to any other constitutional provision**, and nothing in this Section states the measure of any such right.\n\n**(h-1) Notice before damages beyond nominal.** In an action under this Section, damages other than nominal damages are available only where the custodian **remained in the condition complained of 15 days after written notice** identifying the condition and the person affected. **Notice is not required** where the condition poses a substantial risk of serious harm, where the person has been transferred or released, where notice would expose the person to retaliation, or where the custodian has previously received notice of a substantially similar condition at the same facility. **This subsection conditions a category of damages and nothing else** \u2014 it is not an exhaustion requirement, creates no administrative remedy, does not bar or delay an action, and does not apply to relief in equity, to nominal damages, or to any claim under \u00a7 8-2. \u00a7 5-1(b) is unaffected.\n\n**(i) Severability by class of defendant.** As applied to a custodian that is the United States, an agency or officer of the United States, or a person holding another in custody under a contract or arrangement with the United States, this Section rests on the power of Congress to establish and regulate the institutions of the United States and to govern the conduct of its own officers, employees and contractors. **If this Section is held invalid as applied to a State, a political subdivision of a State, or a person acting under color of the law of a State, it remains in full force as to every custodian described in the preceding sentence**, and the invalidity does not affect any other Section of this Act."},{"anchor":"sec-8-3","heading":"\u00a7 8-3. Conditions to which this Section applies","part":"SECTION 8 \u2014 CONDITIONS OF CONFINEMENT","body":"**(a) Enumerated.** Subsection \u00a7 8-2(a) applies to, among other conditions: medical care; mental-health care; medication and treatment for a diagnosed condition; protection from violence by other persons in custody or by staff; heat, cold, ventilation, and air quality; potable water, sanitation, and hygiene; nutrition; sleep and light; the accommodation of a disability; and access to counsel, to the courts, and to the grievance process.\n\n**(b) Residual.** The enumeration in subsection (a) is not exhaustive. Any other condition of confinement posing a substantial risk of serious harm is within \u00a7 8-2(a), and **no condition is excluded on the ground that it is not enumerated.**"},{"anchor":"sec-8-3a","heading":"\u00a7 8-3A. Medical, dental, and mental-health care","part":"SECTION 8 \u2014 CONDITIONS OF CONFINEMENT","body":"**(a) Duty.** A custodian shall provide each person in custody with medical, dental, and mental-health care **adequate to diagnose and treat the person's serious health needs**, at no cost to the person.\n\n**(b) Timeliness.** The following are requirements of this Section. **A failure to meet paragraph (2) as to an emergent condition, or paragraph (3), is a departure from \u00a7 8-2(a) without further showing**. A failure to meet paragraph (1), paragraph (2) as to a non-emergent condition, or paragraph (4) **is a departure unless the custodian shows that the care was furnished within a reasonable time and that the delay posed no substantial risk of serious harm** \u2014 on which the custodian bears the burden.\n1. **Intake screening** by a qualified health professional within the period set in the schedule to this Act of arrival at a facility;\n2. **Response to a request for care** within the period set in the schedule, and immediate response where the request describes an emergent condition;\n3. **Continuity of a prescribed medication** without interruption on intake, on transfer, and on release, including a supply sufficient to reach the next provider; and\n4. **Referral to a specialist** within the period set in the schedule where one is ordered.\n\nA referral under paragraph (4) may be ordered by any licensed health professional who has examined or treated the person, whether or not employed or engaged by the custodian, and making or furnishing the order is protected activity. The period under paragraph (4) runs from the order, or, where the professional is not employed or engaged by the custodian, from its delivery to the custodian, including its delivery with the person on return from care outside the facility.\n\n**(c) Clinical judgment is exercised by clinicians.** A decision to provide, withhold, delay, modify, or discontinue care shall be made by a licensed health professional acting within the scope of that license. **No officer or employee whose function is custody, security, or administration may override, countermand, or delay a clinical decision**, and a delay attributable to such a person is attributable to the custodian.\n\n**(d) Cost is not a permissible ground.** Neither the cost of care, the availability of appropriations, nor the terms of any contract or reimbursement arrangement is a legitimate institutional objective for purposes of \u00a7 8-2A, or a defense under this Section.\n\n**(e) Language.** A clinical encounter shall be conducted in a language the person understands, through a qualified interpreter where necessary. **Another person in custody shall not be used as an interpreter for a clinical encounter.**\n\n**(f) Chronic conditions, pregnancy, and disability.** Care under subsection (a) includes management of a chronic condition, pregnancy and postpartum care, care related to a substance-use disorder including medication for opioid use disorder, and accommodations required by a disability.\n\n**(g) Records.** A person in custody, and a person the person designates in writing, is entitled to a copy of the person's health records on request, without charge and without conditions.\n\n**(h) How the periods are kept, published.** \u2014 Each agency that holds persons in custody, or that causes persons to be held, shall publish annually, by facility: the number of intake screenings and the share completed within the period under subsection (b)(1); the number of requests for care and the time to response at the median and at the ninetieth percentile; the number of specialist referrals ordered and the time to referral at the median and at the ninetieth percentile; and the number of interruptions of a prescribed medication on intake, transfer and release. The duty is ministerial and enforceable in an action in the nature of mandamus by any person, and no person in custody may be identified.\n\n**(h-1) A pattern is named for what it is, and the publication says what would fix it.** \u2014 Where the publication under subsection (h) shows, for a facility, periods missed in more than the share set in the schedule to this Act, **that pattern is evidence that the facility's clinical staffing is inadequate and shall be stated as such in the publication**, together with **the clinical and support personnel required to meet the periods.** The statement is transmitted to the head of the agency and to the Congress, and renewed annually until the pattern ends.\n\n**(h-2) Who publishes.** \u2014 Subsection (h) applies to every agency of the United States that holds persons in custody or causes them to be held, and to any other custodian holding, on average, more than the number of persons set in the schedule to this Act. A custodian below the threshold furnishes the same information on request to a person in its custody, to that person's counsel, and to the Attorney General, and publishes nothing.\n\n**(h-3) And if it may not reach a State, it still reaches everything else.** \u2014\n1. As applied to an agency of the United States, a person holding custody under an instrument described in \u00a7 8-7(a) with the United States, or a person holding custody that the United States causes to be held as described in \u00a7 8-7(c), subsections (h) through (h-2) rest on the power of Congress to establish and regulate the institutions of the United States and to govern the conduct of its own officers, employees, and contractors, **and Congress would have enacted them as to that custody alone.** If they are held invalid as applied to a State, a political subdivision of a State, or a person acting under color of State law, they remain in full force as to every custodian described in this paragraph.\n2. As to a State or local custodian, subsections (h) through (h-2) apply from enactment on each of two independent grounds: (A) section 5 of the Fourteenth Amendment, upon the findings in \u00a7 0-2(6), as a means of detecting and preventing deprivations of the care this Section requires; and (B) as a condition on any Federal funds that custodian receives for the custody of persons, to the extent the Congress may so condition them.\n3. Each ground in paragraph (2) is severable from the other, and **the invalidity of one supports no inference as to the other.**\n\n**(i) Where the referral period lapses, the person does not keep waiting.** \u2014 Where a referral ordered under subsection (b)(4) is not furnished within the period set in the schedule, **the referral is authorized by operation of this subsection and shall be furnished by a provider outside the facility at the custodian's expense**, on the order of the professional who ordered the referral and without any further approval by any officer. No cost of care so furnished may be charged to the person."},{"anchor":"sec-8-3b","heading":"\u00a7 8-3B. Standards of confinement have the force of law","part":"SECTION 8 \u2014 CONDITIONS OF CONFINEMENT","body":"**(a) Promulgation required.** The head of each agency that holds persons in custody, or that causes persons to be held, shall **promulgate by regulation** standards governing the conditions in which they are held, addressing at a minimum each condition enumerated in \u00a7 8-3(a) and the care required by \u00a7 8-3A.\n\n**(b) Guidance is not a standard.** A policy, manual, directive, performance standard, detention standard, or other issuance that has not been promulgated by regulation and published **is not a standard for purposes of this Section**, and no such issuance may be relied upon by a custodian as establishing compliance with this Act.\n\n**(c) Mandatory term.** Standards promulgated under subsection (a) shall be a term of every instrument described in \u00a7 8-7(a), and **\u00a7 8-7(b) applies to their omission**.\n\n**(d) Default where none is promulgated.** Where an agency has not promulgated standards under subsection (a) within one year of the effective date of this Section, **the standards published by the Bureau of Prisons governing the confinement of persons in its custody apply by operation of law** to every facility in which that agency holds or causes persons to be held, and are enforceable under this Act as though the agency had promulgated them. Where the agency has previously applied a published standard more protective of the person than the corresponding Bureau standard, **that standard applies instead as to that subject.** **No determination, election, or action by any officer is required for this subsection to operate**, and the duties of \u00a7 8-2, \u00a7 8-2A and \u00a7 8-3A apply whether or not any standard is in force.\n\n**(e) A floor, not a ceiling.** Compliance with a standard is **not a defense** to a claim under \u00a7 8-2 or \u00a7 8-2A. A condition may satisfy every applicable standard and still violate this Act.\n\n**(f) Non-compliance is evidence.** A failure to comply with an applicable standard is evidence of the condition and of the custodian's knowledge of it under \u00a7 8-2(c). **This subsection creates no separate cause of action for non-compliance with a standard**, the standards being enforceable through the duties this Act states."},{"anchor":"sec-8-3c","heading":"\u00a7 8-3C. Death and serious injury in custody","part":"SECTION 8 \u2014 CONDITIONS OF CONFINEMENT","body":"**(a) Independent review.** Where a person dies in custody, or suffers serious bodily injury in custody, the agency shall cause a review to be conducted **by a person who is not an officer, employee, or contractor of the facility, of its operator, or of the component that holds the person.**\n\n**(b) Findings published.** The reviewer shall make written findings of fact stating what occurred, what conditions or acts contributed, and what was known to the custodian beforehand. **The findings shall be published within 180 days**, withholding only such information as is prohibited by statute from disclosure, with an index of what is withheld and the statutory basis.\n\n**(c) Preservation.** Upon a death or serious bodily injury in custody, every record relating to the person's custody, care, housing, and condition \u2014 including recordings, logs, health records, and grievances \u2014 **is preserved by operation of law** for the period set in the schedule to this Act. **No demand, order, or notice is required.**\n\n**(d) Notice.** The agency shall notify the person's next of kin, and any counsel of record, within 24 hours, and shall furnish the findings under subsection (b) on publication.\n\n**(e) The review is not a condition of any remedy.** No action under this Act is barred, delayed, tolled, or affected by the pendency, completion, or content of a review under this Section."},{"anchor":"sec-8-3d","heading":"\u00a7 8-3D. Access to counsel, to courts, and to family","part":"SECTION 8 \u2014 CONDITIONS OF CONFINEMENT","body":"**(a) Confidential communication with counsel.** A custodian shall provide each person in custody a reasonable opportunity to communicate with counsel **in confidence**, in person, by telephone, and in writing. A communication with counsel shall not be monitored, recorded, read, or retained, and **any such communication obtained by a custodian is inadmissible in any proceeding, and shall be sequestered from every officer and employee of the custodian and of any party adverse to the person.** It shall be destroyed when it is no longer subject to a duty of preservation, and **a duty of preservation is not a ground to review, use, or disclose it.**\n\n**(b) Cost.** Communication with counsel shall be provided at no cost to the person or to counsel.\n\n**(c) Legal materials and assistance.** A custodian shall provide access to legal materials, to a means of preparing and filing documents, and to assistance for a person unable to use them without help.\n\n**(d) Transfer does not sever representation.** Before transferring a person who is represented, or who has a pending application, motion, or proceeding, the custodian shall **give counsel and the person notice, and a reasonable opportunity to be heard**, and shall record the reason for the transfer. **A transfer made without that notice is a condition to which \u00a7 8-2A applies**, and the person's representation, filing deadlines, and pending proceedings are unaffected by the transfer.\n\n**(e) Family contact.** A custodian shall provide a reasonable opportunity to communicate with family members, and shall not condition it on any waiver."},{"anchor":"sec-8-3e","heading":"\u00a7 8-3E. Care is a condition of the authority to hold","part":"SECTION 8 \u2014 CONDITIONS OF CONFINEMENT","body":"**(a) Condition on the authority.** A custodian may hold a person in custody **only while it has capacity to furnish that person the care \u00a7 8-3A requires**, in the custody where the person is held or under \u00a7 8-3A(i). Where it lacks that capacity as to a person, **the person shall be transferred or released on conditions** as this Section provides.\n\n**(b) What establishes a lack of capacity.** A custodian lacks capacity as to a person, without further showing, where \u2014\n1. a treating clinician has recorded that care the person's serious health needs require cannot be furnished in the custody where the person is held, and the care has not been furnished, there or elsewhere, within the period set in the schedule to this Act after the record was made;\n2. a referral authorized by \u00a7 8-3A(i) has not been furnished within the period set in the schedule after it was authorized;\n3. a prescribed medication has been interrupted as to the person for longer than the period set in the schedule; or\n4. a request for care has not been met by a face-to-face contact with a qualified health professional within the period set in the schedule after the period under \u00a7 8-3A(b)(2) has run.\n\nA lack of capacity under paragraphs (1) through (4) is also established, without awaiting the period, when the custodian determines, or the scheduling of the care shows, that the care will not be furnished within it. A record under paragraph (1) may be made by any licensed health professional who has examined or treated the person, whether or not employed or engaged by the custodian, and making or furnishing that record is protected activity. For purposes of paragraph (3), a medication is interrupted where it is discontinued or substituted otherwise than on an individualized clinical determination recorded with its reason, and neither cost, a formulary, nor the medication's availability in the custody is such a reason.\n\n**Any other showing that a custodian lacks capacity remains available**, and a statement in force under \u00a7 8-3A(h-1) for the facility is evidence of a lack of capacity as to each person held there whose care under \u00a7 8-3A(b) is outstanding. A person's informed refusal of care does not establish a lack of capacity as to that care. The custodian bears the burden of establishing that a refusal was the person's own, was made after the person was told what care was offered and the risk of declining it, and was recorded when made.\n\n**(c) Care, then transfer, then release.** Within the period set in the schedule after a lack of capacity is established as to a person, the custodian shall \u2014\n1. furnish the care, which ends the lack;\n2. transfer the person to custody in which the care will be furnished, **including a hospital or other health facility**, at the custodian's expense; or\n3. failing either, apply to the court described in subsection (d)(1) for the person's release on conditions, with notice to the person and to counsel.\n\nA transfer under paragraph (2) is subject to \u00a7 8-3D(d), except that notice may follow the transfer where waiting would itself pose a substantial risk of serious harm. **The duty to apply under paragraph (3) is ministerial and enforceable in an action in the nature of mandamus by the person or by any person acting on the person's behalf**, and the person may apply on the same ground. A period under subsection (b) or (c) is **not restarted** by a transfer, by a return under subsection (d)(5), or by a change of custodian, and a lack of capacity established as to a person continues as to each custodian that thereafter holds the person until the care is furnished. Care furnished under paragraph (1) ends the lack only while it continues as the treating clinician prescribed.\n\n**(d) Release on conditions.**\n1. **Who sets the conditions.** The conditions are set by the court that ordered, or has authority to set conditions of release from, the custody in which the person is held, or, where no court did, by the district court of the United States for the district in which the person is held, or, where the person is held outside every judicial district, by the United States District Court for the District of Columbia.\n2. **The conditions.** The person shall be released on **the least restrictive conditions that will reasonably assure the person's appearance and the safety of any other person**, which may include supervision, residence at a stated place or in a treatment facility, home confinement, and electronic monitoring.\n3. **Where no condition suffices.** Where the court finds, **by clear and convincing evidence**, that no condition of release will reasonably assure the safety of any other person, the person shall instead be held in a hospital or other health facility in which the care will be furnished, under such security as the court orders, at the custodian's expense.\n4. **Credit.** Time on release, or in a facility, under this subsection is **credited toward the sentence or period of custody as though served in the custody from which the person was released.**\n5. **Return.** On a finding that the lack of capacity has been remedied and cannot reasonably be expected to recur, on which the custodian bears the burden, the court may order the person returned to the custody from which the person was released.\n6. **Care during release.** The custodian from which the person was released bears the cost of the care that established the lack, without charge to the person, until that care is complete or the release ends.\n\n**(e) Enforcement.** A person held in violation of subsection (a) shall be treated as **in custody in violation of the laws of the United States** for purposes of sections 2241(c)(3) and 2254(a) of title 28. Where a period stated in subsection (b) or (c) has run, State corrective process shall be treated as ineffective to protect the rights of the applicant for purposes of section 2254(b)(1)(B)(ii) of that title, unless a State court has heard and decided an application on the same ground within the period set in the schedule after it was filed. An application under this subsection shall be treated as challenging the fact of confinement. For purposes of section 2254(d)(1) of title 28, the terms of this Section are clearly established Federal law. An application under this subsection is not a second or successive application within the meaning of section 2244(b) of title 28. Neither such an application nor an application, order, or action under subsection (c) or (d) shall be treated as **a civil action with respect to prison conditions** within the meaning of section 3626(g)(2) of title 18, and \u00a7 5-1(e)(3) does not apply to any of them.\n\n**(f) What this Section does not do.** A transfer or release under this Section does not vacate, reduce, or otherwise alter any conviction, sentence, or order of detention, is not a modification of a term of imprisonment within the meaning of section 3582(c) of title 18, and \u00a7 5-3 is unaffected. \u00a7 8-3A(d) applies to every duty under this Section, and \u00a7 8-3A(b)(3) applies on release. As to a person detained under a provision to which section 301 of the Day in Court Act applies, that section and this Section each apply, and **neither limits the other.**\n\n**(g) Counts.** Each agency and custodian that publishes under \u00a7 8-3A(h) shall publish monthly, by facility, the number of persons as to whom a lack of capacity was established, and the number transferred, released on conditions, and held under subsection (d)(3), under this Section. The duty is ministerial and enforceable as \u00a7 8-3A(h) provides.\n\n**(h) Severability by class of custodian, and three grounds as to State custody.**\n1. As applied to a custodian that is the United States, an agency or officer of the United States, a person holding custody under an instrument described in \u00a7 8-7(a) with the United States, or a person holding custody that the United States causes to be held as described in \u00a7 8-7(c), this Section rests on the power of Congress to establish and regulate the institutions of the United States and to govern the conduct of its own officers, employees, and contractors, **and Congress would have enacted it as to that custody alone.** If this Section is held invalid as applied to a State, a political subdivision of a State, or a person acting under color of State law, it remains in full force as to every custodian described in this paragraph.\n2. As to a State or local custodian, this Section applies from its enactment on each of three independent grounds: (A) \u00a7 13-2(c)(2); (B) **where the failure to furnish the care violates the Eighth or the Fourteenth Amendment**, as a remedy for that violation; and (C) as a condition on any Federal funds that custodian receives for the custody of persons, to the extent the Congress may so condition them.\n3. Each ground in paragraph (2) is severable from the others, and **the invalidity of one supports no inference as to another.**"},{"anchor":"sec-8-4","heading":"\u00a7 8-4. Isolation","part":"SECTION 8 \u2014 CONDITIONS OF CONFINEMENT","body":"**(a) Condition on the authority.** A custodian may hold a person in isolation for a period exceeding **fifteen consecutive days** only if, before the sixteenth day and at intervals of not more than fifteen days thereafter, an officer of the agency has made and recorded a determination, **as to that person**, stating the specific facts making continued isolation necessary, the less restrictive measures considered and why each was inadequate, and the date on which isolation is to end.\n\n**(b) Isolation defined.** **Isolation** is confinement in a cell or other space for twenty-two hours or more in a twenty-four-hour period without meaningful human contact, **however the practice is designated by the custodian.**\n\n**(c) Per se departures.** Holding a person in isolation departs from \u00a7 8-2(a), without regard to duration, where the person is under eighteen years of age, is pregnant or within eight weeks postpartum, or has a serious mental illness \u2014 except where isolation is the least restrictive means of preventing an immediate risk of physical harm and continues only so long as that risk persists.\n\n**(d) Congress's own determination.** The period stated in subsection (a) is the determination of Congress as to when isolation becomes prolonged. It is not a finding as to the requirements of any provision of the Constitution or of any other body of law."},{"anchor":"sec-8-5","heading":"\u00a7 8-5. Threshold","part":"SECTION 8 \u2014 CONDITIONS OF CONFINEMENT","body":"A condition that causes only transient discomfort, inconvenience, or distress, and that a reasonable custodian would not expect to result in lasting physical or psychological harm, does not pose a substantial risk of serious harm. **Nothing in this Section requires that a person in custody first suffer physical injury**, and \u00a7 5-1(a) applies to every claim under this Section."},{"anchor":"sec-8-6","heading":"\u00a7 8-6. Relation to other law","part":"SECTION 8 \u2014 CONDITIONS OF CONFINEMENT","body":"**(a) Constitutional claims preserved.** Nothing in this Section limits, displaces, or supplies the content of any right secured by the Constitution, and no court may treat this Section as stating the measure of any such right.\n\n**(a-1) Persons not held for a criminal violation.** A person held in civil or immigration detention, or otherwise than by reason of an accusation, conviction, sentence, or adjudication of delinquency for a violation of criminal law, **is not a prisoner within the meaning of section 1997e(h) of title 42**, and no requirement of the Prison Litigation Reform Act of 1995 applies to such a person. **This subsection states what that Act already provides**.\n\n**(b) A more protective standard governs.** Where any other provision of Federal law imposes on a custodian a duty more protective of a person in custody than this Section, that provision applies."},{"anchor":"sec-8-7","heading":"\u00a7 8-7. Custody performed under contract","part":"SECTION 8 \u2014 CONDITIONS OF CONFINEMENT","body":"**(a) Condition on the authority.** A governmental employer may enter into, renew, extend, or make payment under a contract, agreement, or arrangement under which another person holds a person in custody **only if** the instrument provides that the custodian \u2014\n1. shall comply with this Section and with every other provision of this Act applicable to a custodian;\n2. **consents to suit** under this Act in the district courts of the United States, and waives any defense that it is not a governmental employer, is not a State actor, or does not act under color of law or authority;\n3. shall make, preserve, and furnish the same records, determinations, and reports this Act requires of a governmental employer performing the same function; and\n4. shall not be indemnified by any governmental employer for punitive damages, or for conduct the trier of fact found to have been undertaken with knowledge of its unlawfulness.\n\n**(b) Omission is not a defense.** Where an instrument does not contain a term required by subsection (a), **the term applies to the custodian as though it were set out in the instrument**, and neither the omission, nor any term purporting to limit the custodian's liability, nor any provision of the instrument allocating risk between the parties, is a defense to any person's claim under this Act.\n\n**(c) Anti-substitution.** Where a governmental employer causes a person to be held in custody otherwise than under an instrument described in subsection (a) \u2014 including by designation, transfer, reimbursement, intergovernmental agreement, or any other means \u2014 **the conditions of subsection (a) apply according to their terms** to the person holding that custody.\n\n**(d) Publication.** A governmental employer shall publish each instrument described in subsection (a), and each amendment to one, withholding only such portions as are prohibited by statute from disclosure, together with an index of what is withheld and the statutory basis."},{"anchor":"sec-8-8","heading":"\u00a7 8-8. Construction of this Section","part":"SECTION 8 \u2014 CONDITIONS OF CONFINEMENT","body":"**(a) These duties are statutory.** Sections 8-1 through 8-7 state duties **created by this Act**. Their existence and content do not depend on the construction any court has given to the Eighth Amendment, to the Due Process Clause of the Fifth or Fourteenth Amendment, or to any other constitutional provision.\n\n**(b) Deference doctrines displaced by name.** Congress has considered and **expressly displaces**, as to a claim under this Section, the deference to the judgment of custodial officials applied under *Turner v. Safley* and *Bell v. Wolfish* and their progeny, and any like doctrine however denominated. **This Section states a statutory standard, and no deference to the custodian's judgment applies in determining whether it is met.** A custodian's stated objective is evidence and **is not conclusive**; \u00a7 8-2A(d-1) fixes what may be asserted, not what must be accepted.\n\n**(c) Mens-rea glosses displaced by name.** No claim under this Section requires a showing of deliberate indifference, subjective awareness, reckless disregard, wantonness, malice, an intent to punish, or a culpable state of mind of any kind. **\u00a7 8-2(b) states the standard and no other applies.** A defense, element, or limitation displaced by this subsection may not be reintroduced as an element of the plaintiff's case, a pleading requirement, a standard of proof, a component of the merits, or a limitation on remedy.\n\n**(d) Causation.** A custodian is liable where the condition was **a but-for cause** of the injury. **No heightened causal standard applies**, and the phrases \"moving force,\" \"affirmative link,\" \"proximate cause,\" and \"direct cause\" state no requirement of this Section.\n\n**(e) Conditions are assessed together.** Conditions shall be assessed **cumulatively as well as individually**. That each condition considered alone would not pose a substantial risk of serious harm is not a ground for judgment where their combined effect does, and **a court may not require a plaintiff to identify a single condition as the sole source of the risk.**\n\n**(f) Medical glosses displaced.** That a treating clinician exercised judgment, that another clinician would have chosen differently, that care was eventually provided, or that a delay produced no separately provable additional injury, **is not a defense** to a claim under \u00a7 8-3A. **A delay that itself prolongs pain, or that a reasonable clinician would not have permitted, is a departure**, and the absence of a worsened diagnosis does not establish its absence.\n\n**(g) Terms defined.** For purposes of this Section \u2014 **\"custody\"** means any restraint on liberty under which a person is not free to leave, however denominated and wherever carried out, including a lockup, cell, holding room, vehicle, tent, ship, aircraft, hotel, or facility of any kind, and whether operated by a governmental employer or by another. **\"Condition of confinement\"** means anything to which a person in custody is subjected, or of which the person is deprived, by reason of being in custody, **including an omission**. **\"Serious harm\"** includes physical injury, significant pain, the material worsening of a health condition, and psychological injury, and **is not limited to permanent or diagnosable injury**. **\"Care\"** includes diagnosis, treatment, medication, and follow-up.\n\n**(h) Remedies.** The remedies for a violation of this Section are those provided by Sections 2 and 3 and by \u00a7 8-3E, and relief in equity **shall** issue on a finding of violation and a showing that the violation is continuing or reasonably likely to recur. **The continuation of a condition after a finding that it violates this Section is irreparable injury**, and a custodian asserting that the balance of equities or the public interest weighs against relief bears the burden. **Nothing in this subsection directs how any question of fact or law is to be decided.**\n\n**(i) Standing and mootness.** **Release, transfer, or a change in custodial status does not moot a claim** for damages, and does not moot a claim for equitable relief where the custodian remains free to resume the condition as to that person or others; the custodian bears the burden of establishing that it cannot reasonably be expected to recur.\n\n**(j) Severability, and its direction.** If \u00a7 8-2A is held invalid, \u00a7 8-2 and \u00a7\u00a7 8-3 through 8-3E remain in force. If \u00a7 8-3B is held invalid, \u00a7 8-2, \u00a7 8-2A and \u00a7 8-3A are unaffected and enforceable without any promulgated standard. **If any subsection of this Section is held invalid as applied to a State, a political subdivision, or a person acting under color of State law, it remains in full force as to the United States, its agencies and officers, and persons holding custody under contract or arrangement with the United States** \u2014 Congress would have enacted each of these provisions as to federal custody alone."},{"anchor":"sec-9-1","heading":"\u00a7 9-1. Scope and definitions","part":"SECTION 9 \u2014 USE OF FORCE","body":"**(a) Application.** This Section applies to the use of force by a covered defendant in the course of an arrest, an investigatory stop, the execution of process, the maintenance of custody, the control of a crowd, or any other exercise of law-enforcement or custodial authority.\n\n**(b) Force.** \"Force\" means physical force applied to the body of a person, whether by hand, by instrument, by animal, by chemical or electrical means, or by projectile.\n\n**(c) Deadly force.** \"Deadly force\" means force that a reasonable officer would recognize as creating a substantial risk of death or serious bodily injury, **whether or not death or serious bodily injury results.**\n\n**(d) Seizure by force.** The application of force to the body of a person **with intent to restrain that person is a seizure**, whether or not the person is subdued, submits, is detained, or escapes, and **whether the force is applied by hand or by projectile.** The failure of an application of force to accomplish restraint does not remove it from this Section.\n\n**(e) Custody under contract.** Section 8-7 applies to this Section, and a person performing a function described in this Section under contract with, or at the direction of, a governmental employer is subject to it on the terms stated there.","partHeads":[{"heading":"SECTION 9 \u2014 USE OF FORCE","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-9-2","heading":"\u00a7 9-2. Standard for the use of force","part":"SECTION 9 \u2014 USE OF FORCE","body":"**(a) Necessity.** A covered defendant may use force only where, and only for so long as, force is **necessary to accomplish a legitimate law-enforcement or custodial objective**, and only in a **degree proportionate** to that objective and to the resistance or threat actually presented.\n\n**(a-1) The necessity inquiry.** Whether force was necessary and proportionate is determined on the circumstances known to the covered defendant at the time, and the inquiry includes \u2014\n1. the **severity of the offense** suspected, and whether it was a felony, a misdemeanor, or a civil violation;\n2. whether the person posed an **immediate threat** to any person, and the nature of that threat;\n3. whether the person was **actively resisting or attempting to evade** custody, as distinct from failing to comply promptly, questioning the officer, or remaining passive;\n4. whether a **less severe means** \u2014 including verbal direction, waiting, withdrawal, or summoning additional officers \u2014 reasonably appeared sufficient;\n5. the **duration** of the force, and whether it continued after the objective was accomplished; and\n6. any **apparent age, disability, injury, or medical or mental-health crisis** of the person, where it was apparent or communicated to the covered defendant.\n\n**(b) Deadly force.** A covered defendant may use deadly force only where **necessary to prevent death or serious bodily injury to that defendant or to another person**, and only where **no less severe means reasonably appears sufficient** to prevent it. The prevention of escape is not, standing alone, a ground for deadly force.\n\n**(c) Warning.** Before using deadly force a covered defendant shall give a warning of the intent to use it, **where doing so is feasible and would not itself create the risk the force is used to prevent.** Where no warning was given, the absence of feasibility is an element of the defendant's justification.\n\n**(d) The whole encounter is the subject of the inquiry.** Whether force complied with this Section is determined **on the totality of the circumstances**, which includes **the covered defendant's own conduct before the force was applied**, where that conduct created or increased the danger the force was used to meet. **No inquiry under this Section may be confined to the moment immediately preceding the application of force.**\n\n**(e) Prohibited applications.** Force is not necessary within the meaning of subsection (a), and its use violates this Section, where it is applied \u2014\n1. to a person who is **restrained and not resisting**;\n2. **to punish**, to retaliate for speech or for a person's refusal to answer, or to compel a statement;\n3. as a **chokehold or other means of restricting the airway or blood flow to the brain**, except where deadly force is permitted by subsection (b); or\n4. **against a person solely because that person is recording, observing, or verbally objecting** to the exercise of official authority.\n\n**(f) Compliance with policy is not a defense.** Conformity to an agency policy, training, or directive **is not a defense** to a violation of this Section, and does not establish that force was necessary or proportionate.\n\n**(f-1) Where the policy is the cause.** Where the trier of fact finds that a covered defendant used force in **conformity with a policy, training, or directive of the governmental employer**, and that the defendant did not know the force was unlawful \u2014\n1. **punitive damages may not be awarded against that defendant**; and\n2. the finding is evidence that the deprivation was caused by the policy, for purposes of **\u00a7 3-1**.\n\n**Liability for a policy that requires force departing from \u00a7 9-2 belongs to the employer that adopted it**, and this subsection does not reduce the compensation owed to the injured person by any defendant.\n\n**(g) The standard is stated here.** The standard of conduct required by this Section is the standard stated **in this Section**. It is **not** to be construed by reference to, or limited by, the construction any court has given to any constitutional provision addressing the use of force, or to the availability of any immunity."},{"anchor":"sec-9-3","heading":"\u00a7 9-3. Duty to render aid","part":"SECTION 9 \u2014 USE OF FORCE","body":"**(a) The duty.** Where a covered defendant has used force causing injury, or is present where force used by another has caused injury, that defendant shall, **as soon as doing so does not create a substantial risk to any person** \u2014 determine whether the injured person is alive and in need of medical assistance, summon emergency medical assistance, and render such aid as the defendant is trained and equipped to provide.\n\n**(b) The duty is independent of justification.** The duty stated in subsection (a) attaches **whether or not the force was permitted by \u00a7 9-2.** A determination that the force was lawful is **not a defense** to a violation of this Section, and a violation of this Section is actionable under this Act **standing alone.**\n\n**(c) Record.** The time at which assistance was summoned, by whom, and the time of its arrival shall be recorded and preserved. Where a covered defendant asserts that subsection (a) excused delay, **the defendant bears the burden of establishing the risk relied upon.**"},{"anchor":"sec-9-4","heading":"\u00a7 9-4. Absence of a required recording","part":"SECTION 9 \u2014 USE OF FORCE","body":"**(a) Rule.** Where a recording of an encounter was required by statute, regulation, or agency policy, and the recording is **absent, incomplete, or has not been preserved**, the party whose duty it was to make or preserve it **bears the burden of persuasion as to any fact the recording would have depicted.**\n\n**(b) Fault.** Subsection (a) does not apply where the party establishes that the absence resulted from **equipment failure or circumstances not attributable to the fault of that party or of any person acting on its behalf.**\n\n**(c) Intentional destruction.** Where the court finds, **by clear and convincing evidence**, that a recording was destroyed, altered, or withheld **intentionally**, or that a person was directed to destroy or alter one, **the facts the recording would have depicted are established** against the party responsible and against any person who participated in or directed the destruction, in addition to any other sanction available. **This subsection does not operate against an individual defendant who neither participated in nor directed it**, as to whom subsection (a) applies.\n\n**(d) Symmetry.** This Section applies **according to its terms to any party**, including a plaintiff, who was required to make or preserve a recording within that party's control. Nothing in this Section limits the use of a recording to establish that force was **necessary and proportionate.**\n\n**(e) No adverse inference from lawful recording.** The fact that a person recorded, or attempted to record, an encounter **supports no inference** as to that person's conduct or credibility."},{"anchor":"sec-10-1","heading":"\u00a7 10-1. The right","part":"SECTION 10 \u2014 OBSERVING AND RECORDING OFFICIAL CONDUCT","body":"**(a) The right.** Every person has the right to **observe, photograph, and record** a covered defendant performing official duties, in a public place or in any other place where that person is lawfully present.\n\n**(b) What the right does not include.** This Section confers no right to **forcibly impede, obstruct, or physically interfere** with the performance of official duties, to enter a place where the person is not lawfully present, or to disregard an order that is lawful under subsection (c).\n\n**(c) Orders restricting observation.** An order to move, to disperse, or to cease recording is lawful for purposes of subsection (b) only where it is **necessary to address an identified and immediate hazard to a person's safety or to the integrity of a scene**, and only for so long as that necessity continues. **An order that restricts recording while permitting continued presence is not lawful within the meaning of subsection (b)**, and neither is an order applied to a person recording but not to others similarly situated. **This subsection does not apply where the act of recording is itself the identified hazard** \u2014 including where illumination would reveal a position or impair vision \u2014 and where the covered defendant states that reason at the time and it is recorded.\n\n**(d) Observation is not a ground for action.** Peaceful observation or recording, **without more**, is not reasonable suspicion, is not probable cause, and does not justify a stop, a detention, a search, an order to disperse, an arrest, or any use of force. **Proximity, persistence, and the recording of an officer's identity are not \"more\" within the meaning of this subsection.**","partHeads":[{"heading":"SECTION 10 \u2014 OBSERVING AND RECORDING OFFICIAL CONDUCT","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-10-2","heading":"\u00a7 10-2. Devices and recordings","part":"SECTION 10 \u2014 OBSERVING AND RECORDING OFFICIAL CONDUCT","body":"**(a) No seizure without process.** A covered defendant shall not seize a device used to observe or record official conduct, or the recording it contains, **except under a warrant**, or where seizure is necessary to prevent the imminent destruction of evidence of a crime unrelated to the recording of the covered defendant. Where a device is seized, it shall be **returned promptly**, and any copy retained only as the warrant or an order of a court permits.\n\n**(b) No compelled deletion or access.** A covered defendant shall not **delete, or direct or request any person to delete**, a recording of official conduct, and shall not demand that a person unlock a device, display a recording, or explain why the person is recording, as a condition of remaining present or of being released.\n\n**(c) Destruction.** Deletion of a recording of official conduct by a covered defendant, or at that defendant's direction, is **intentional destruction within the meaning of \u00a7 9-4(c).**"},{"anchor":"sec-11-1","heading":"\u00a7 11-1. Display of identification","part":"SECTION 11 \u2014 IDENTIFICATION","body":"**(a) The requirement.** A covered defendant exercising or asserting coercive authority in a place open to the public shall display, **visibly and legibly** \u2014 the name of the employing agency, and an **individual identifier** unique to that defendant, which may be a name or a number.\n\n**(b) Vehicles.** A vehicle used to stop, transport, or detain a person shall bear **the identification of the employing agency and a registration plate**, except where a court has authorized its use unmarked for a specified investigation.\n\n**(c) Face coverings.** This Section does not prohibit a face covering worn for a **tactical, medical, or environmental** reason. A face covering **does not excuse** the requirements of subsection (a).","partHeads":[{"heading":"SECTION 11 \u2014 IDENTIFICATION","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-11-2","heading":"\u00a7 11-2. Identification on demand","part":"SECTION 11 \u2014 IDENTIFICATION","body":"On request by a person subjected to, or witnessing, the exercise of coercive authority, a covered defendant shall **state the employing agency and the individual identifier**. Where a person is seized, the identification shall be given **at or before the time of the seizure** unless doing so would create an immediate risk to a person's safety, in which case it shall be given as soon as that risk abates."},{"anchor":"sec-11-3","heading":"\u00a7 11-3. Accommodation, not exemption","part":"SECTION 11 \u2014 IDENTIFICATION","body":"Where an individual covered defendant is subject to a **documented and particularized threat**, the employing agency may substitute **a number for that defendant's name**. The agency shall maintain the correspondence between the number and the person, and shall **disclose it on the request of a person asserting a claim under this Act** or on the order of a court. **No agency may withhold identification as to a class, a unit, an operation, or a category of duty.**"},{"anchor":"sec-11-4","heading":"\u00a7 11-4. Identification is a condition of the authority","part":"SECTION 11 \u2014 IDENTIFICATION","body":"The authority of a covered defendant to **stop, detain, search, arrest, or use force** in a place open to the public is **conditioned on compliance with \u00a7\u00a7 11-1 and 11-2.** A covered defendant who acts without complying **violates this Section**, and the violation is actionable under this Act **whether or not the underlying action was otherwise lawful.** **A violation of this Section does not invalidate an arrest, a search, or a seizure, and creates no remedy of suppression in any proceeding.** The remedy for a violation of this Section is the remedy this Act provides."},{"anchor":"sec-11-5","heading":"\u00a7 11-5. Where the individual cannot be identified","part":"SECTION 11 \u2014 IDENTIFICATION","body":"**(a) Duty to identify on demand.** A governmental employer shall, within **30 days** of a written request by a person asserting a claim under this Act, identify **each covered defendant present at** the encounter described, and the agency and unit of each.\n\n**(b) Pleading.** A claim under this Act may be brought against a governmental employer and against **covered defendants whose identities are unknown**, described by their conduct. An amendment substituting a named defendant **relates back** to the filing of the original complaint.\n\n**(c) Limitation tolled.** The period stated in \u00a7 5-4 is **tolled** from the date of a request under subsection (a) until the employer complies."},{"anchor":"sec-12-1","heading":"\u00a7 12-1. Consent must be voluntary","part":"SECTION 12 \u2014 CONSENT","body":"Consent to a search, to an entry, or to the taking of any other action that would otherwise require a warrant or other legal authority is effective only where it is **given voluntarily**, and the **party asserting consent bears the burden of proving it** by clear and convincing evidence. Consent is not established by **acquiescence to a claim of authority**, by silence, or by the failure to object.","partHeads":[{"heading":"SECTION 12 \u2014 CONSENT","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-12-2","heading":"\u00a7 12-2. Consent obtained by material falsehood","part":"SECTION 12 \u2014 CONSENT","body":"Consent is **not voluntary**, and is not consent, where it was obtained by a **false statement** concerning \u2014\n1. the **identity of the agency** or the authority under which the covered defendant acts;\n2. the **purpose of the entry, search, or action** sought; or\n3. the **existence of a warrant, order, or legal obligation** to comply,\n\n**where the falsehood was material** to the decision to consent. A statement is material where a reasonable person in the consenting person's position would have attached importance to it in deciding whether to consent. **Nothing in this Section prohibits deception that is not directed at obtaining consent.**"},{"anchor":"sec-12-3","heading":"\u00a7 12-3. Notice of the right to refuse","part":"SECTION 12 \u2014 CONSENT","body":"Before seeking consent, a covered defendant shall **inform the person that consent may be refused** and that refusal will not itself be treated as evidence of wrongdoing. **Consent given without that notice is not effective**, except where the person's own statement or conduct establishes actual knowledge of the right to refuse. **The notice may be given orally**, and a recording of the encounter establishing that it was given satisfies this Section."},{"anchor":"sec-12-4","heading":"\u00a7 12-4. Refusal","part":"SECTION 12 \u2014 CONSENT","body":"A person's **refusal to consent**, or withdrawal of consent, is **not reasonable suspicion, not probable cause, and not a ground** for a stop, a detention, a search, an arrest, or the use of force, and supports **no inference** as to that person's conduct."},{"anchor":"sec-12-5","heading":"\u00a7 12-5. Scope and withdrawal","part":"SECTION 12 \u2014 CONSENT","body":"Consent extends only to **what was described** when it was sought, and to the places and things a reasonable person would understand that description to include. Consent may be **withdrawn at any time**, and action taken after withdrawal is without consent."},{"anchor":"sec-12-6","heading":"\u00a7 12-6. Authority to consent","part":"SECTION 12 \u2014 CONSENT","body":"Consent given by a person **without actual authority** over the place or thing is not consent. Where a covered defendant reasonably believed such authority existed, that belief is a defense **only where the defendant made reasonable inquiry into the basis of the authority and recorded the basis relied upon** before acting."},{"anchor":"sec-12a-0","heading":"\u00a7 12A-0. Purpose; definitions","part":"SECTION 12A \u2014 ADMINISTRATIVE COMPENSATION FOR SMALL VIOLATIONS","body":"**(a) Purpose.** The purpose of this Section is to provide compensation for violations of the rights created by Sections 8 through 12 that are real but too small to support a civil action, **so that the existence of a remedy does not depend on the size of the harm.**\n\n**(b) Claimant.** The individual against whom the conduct was directed, or the personal representative of such an individual who has died or is incapacitated.\n\n**(c) Employing agency.** The agency of the United States that employed, or engaged under contract, the person whose conduct is the subject of the claim. Where more than one agency qualifies, the agency with which the claim is filed is the employing agency, and it may seek contribution from any other.\n\n**(d) Conduct.** An act or an omission, whether or not any person is identified as responsible for it.\n\n**(e) Construction.** A term used in this Section and defined elsewhere in this Act has the meaning given it there.","partHeads":[{"heading":"SECTION 12A \u2014 ADMINISTRATIVE COMPENSATION FOR SMALL VIOLATIONS","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-12a-1","heading":"\u00a7 12A-1. The schedule","part":"SECTION 12A \u2014 ADMINISTRATIVE COMPENSATION FOR SMALL VIOLATIONS","body":"**(a)** The Attorney General shall publish a schedule of presumptive amounts payable for violations of the rights created by Sections 8 through 12, by category of violation. **That schedule is referred to in this Section as the published schedule**, and is distinct from the SCHEDULE to this Act.\n\n**(b)** The amounts are those stated in the SCHEDULE to this Act.\n\n**(c) No fault need be shown.** A claimant establishes a claim by establishing that the conduct occurred and that it was of a category of violation set in the schedule to this Act.\n\n**(c-1) The categories do not wait on the Attorney General.** The categories of violation are those set in the schedule to this Act. **A claim under subsection (c) may be established whether or not the published schedule exists**, and the absence, incompleteness, or withdrawal of the published schedule does not suspend, toll, or diminish any right under this Section. **The published schedule may add a category or raise an amount; it may not remove a category set in the schedule to this Act or reduce an amount below it.**\n\n**(d) Revision runs one way.** \u2014 An amount in the schedule may be increased at any time, and may be reduced only at a scheduled review occurring no more often than the period stated in the SCHEDULE, on a published statement of reasons."},{"anchor":"sec-12a-2","heading":"\u00a7 12A-2. Filing, and what happens if nobody answers","part":"SECTION 12A \u2014 ADMINISTRATIVE COMPENSATION FOR SMALL VIOLATIONS","body":"**(a) No counsel required.** A claim is made on a form the employing agency shall accept from any person, and **no claim may be refused for want of counsel, for form, or for any defect the agency can cure from information it holds.**\n\n**(a-1) The existing channel is used.** \u2014 A claim under this Section is made, received, and processed under the procedures established under section 2672 of title 28, United States Code, and the regulations under it, to the extent those procedures apply. **This Section supplies the claim and the schedule; it does not establish a second claims process.**\n\n**(a-3) When a claim must be filed.** A claim shall be filed within the period that would apply to a civil action for the same conduct, determined without regard to any waiver, election, or tolling under this Section. **Filing under this Section tolls that period until the claim is finally determined.**\n\n**(b) Decision within a stated period.** The agency shall allow or deny the claim within the period stated in the SCHEDULE.\n\n**(c) Failure to decide is a decision for the claimant.** Where the agency has not decided within that period, **the claim is allowed in the scheduled amount by operation of this subsection**, and no further action by any officer is required.\n\n**(d)** A denial shall be in writing, shall state the facts on which it rests, and shall be furnished to the claimant.\n\n**(d-1) A denial is not evidence.** \u2014 Neither the filing of a claim under this Section, nor its denial, nor anything stated in the agency's disposition of it, may be admitted or otherwise used in any civil action as evidence that the conduct did not occur, that it was lawful, or that the claimant valued it at the scheduled amount. **A claimant who files and is denied is in the position of a claimant who never filed.**\n\n**(e) Denial rates are published, and that is the point.** \u2014 The counts published under \u00a7 12A-7 shall state allowances and denials separately, by agency.\n\n**(f) Reconsideration, once.** \u2014 A claimant may seek reconsideration of a denial once, within the period stated in the SCHEDULE. **Reconsideration is decided by the Attorney General and not by the employing agency**, and the period for bringing a civil action is tolled while it is pending.\n\n**(h) Minority and incapacity.** A period under this Section does not run against an individual while the individual is under 18 years of age or is incapacitated, and begins to run when that condition ends.\n\n**(i) A knowingly false claim.** A claim made with knowledge that it is false is not payable, and any amount paid on it is recoverable. **No other consequence attaches by reason of this Section**, and an unsuccessful claim made in good faith carries none.\n\n**(j) Claims are not assignable.** A claim under this Section may not be sold, assigned, or transferred before it is allowed, and any purported assignment is void."},{"anchor":"sec-12a-3","heading":"\u00a7 12A-3. Payment","part":"SECTION 12A \u2014 ADMINISTRATIVE COMPENSATION FOR SMALL VIOLATIONS","body":"**(a)** An allowed claim is paid by the employing agency of the person whose conduct is the subject of the claim.\n\n**(b) Permanent appropriation.** There are appropriated such sums as are necessary to pay claims under this Section, without fiscal year limitation. **Payment does not depend on an annual decision to fund it.**\n\n**(b-1) The existing payment mechanism.** \u2014 A claim allowed in an amount within the threshold stated in section 2672 of title 28, United States Code, is paid in the manner provided by that section.\n\n**(c) The tiers are appropriated too.** \u2014 There are appropriated such sums as are necessary for the accreditation, advisors, referral, and attorney consultation required by \u00a7 12A-6, without fiscal year limitation. **The appropriation covers personnel** \u2014 the advisors compensated under \u00a7 12A-6(e-1), the attorneys compensated under \u00a7 12A-6(h-1), and the staff who accredit and train them. Amounts under this subsection are in addition to, and shall not be derived from, amounts appropriated by subsection (b) or otherwise available to pay claimants."},{"anchor":"sec-12a-4","heading":"\u00a7 12A-4. Election of remedies","part":"SECTION 12A \u2014 ADMINISTRATIVE COMPENSATION FOR SMALL VIOLATIONS","body":"**(a)** Accepting payment of a scheduled amount waives any civil action for the same conduct.\n\n**(b)** Commencing a civil action for the conduct waives any claim under this Section for that conduct.\n\n**(b-1) The waiver reaches the conduct, not the label.** \u2014 A waiver under this Section applies to any action arising from the same conduct, **regardless of the form, title, or characterization of the action, the theory pleaded, or the statute or common-law rule invoked.** It does not extend to conduct not compensated under the schedule.\n\n**(b-2) And it reaches no further than that.** \u2014 No waiver under this Section affects a claim by any other person, a claim for conduct occurring on another occasion, or any claim excluded by \u00a7 12A-5.\n\n**(c) Not both, and not neither.** Nothing in this Section limits a person who does neither, and the period for bringing a civil action is tolled while a claim under this Section is pending."},{"anchor":"sec-12a-4a","heading":"\u00a7 12A-4A. Tax treatment and effect on benefits","part":"SECTION 12A \u2014 ADMINISTRATIVE COMPENSATION FOR SMALL VIOLATIONS","body":"**(a) Excluded by force of this subsection.** \u2014 Gross income does not include an amount paid under this Section.\n\n**(c) Conforming amendment.** \u2014 Section 104(a) of that Code is amended by inserting after paragraph (1) the following: **\"(1B) amounts received under section 12A-1 of the Enforceable Rights Act;\"**.\n\n**(d) No offset against benefits.** \u2014 An amount paid under this Section shall not be taken into account as income or as a resource in determining eligibility for, or the amount of, any Federal or federally assisted benefit conditioned on need.\n\n**(e) No reporting that defeats the payment.** \u2014 No amount paid under this Section is subject to information reporting that would characterize it as includible in gross income."},{"anchor":"sec-12a-5","heading":"\u00a7 12A-5. What may never be settled under this Section","part":"SECTION 12A \u2014 ADMINISTRATIVE COMPENSATION FOR SMALL VIOLATIONS","body":"**(a) The floor.** No claim may be made or paid under this Section, and no waiver under \u00a7 12A-4 is effective, with respect to \u2014\n\n**(1)** death;\n\n**(2)** serious bodily injury;\n\n**(3)** a sexual act or sexual contact by a covered defendant;\n\n**(4)** detention beyond the period stated in the SCHEDULE; or\n\n**(5)** loss of a home or of employment.\n\n**(b) These and only these.** The categories in subsection (a) are exclusive, and **the omission of a harm from that subsection is a determination by Congress and not a gap.**\n\n**(c) The floor is categories of harm, never amounts.** No provision of this Section may be construed to permit a harm described in subsection (a) to be brought within the schedule by reason of the amount claimed, the amount offered, or any valuation of the claim.\n\n**(d) Written notice above a stated amount.** Where a claim exceeds the amount stated in the SCHEDULE, the agency shall furnish the claimant, before any waiver is effective, a written statement of what is being waived and of the right to consult an advisor under \u00a7 12A-6."},{"anchor":"sec-12a-6","heading":"\u00a7 12A-6. The advisor","part":"SECTION 12A \u2014 ADMINISTRATIVE COMPENSATION FOR SMALL VIOLATIONS","body":"**(a) Three tiers.** The Attorney General shall provide **online self-service information**, **consultation with an accredited advisor**, and **consultation with a volunteer attorney reached on an advisor's referral**.\n\n**(a-1) The tiers are not one body.** \u2014 The tier described as consultation with an accredited advisor and the tier described as consultation with a volunteer attorney **may not be provided by the same organization**, and neither may be provided by an agency against which claims under this Section are made.\n\n**(b) Accreditation, narrow to this function.** The Attorney General shall accredit advisors, who need not be attorneys. **Accreditation authorizes advice under this Section only, and State law governing the unauthorized practice of law does not apply to advice given within that authorization.**\n\n**(b-1) What accreditation requires.** \u2014 An advisor shall complete training prescribed by the Attorney General, shall certify annually that the advisor remains in compliance with this Section, and is subject to standards of conduct prescribed by the Attorney General. **Accreditation may be withdrawn for a violation of those standards.**\n\n**(c) The duty runs to the claimant.** An advisor's duty is to the claimant, and to no agency, program, or fund.\n\n**(d) Independence.** An advisor may not be employed by, paid by, or subject to direction from the agency against which the claim is made.\n\n**(e) No fee, no interest.** An advisor may not charge a fee, accept anything of value from a claimant, or hold any interest in the outcome of a claim.\n\n**(e-1) How an advisor is paid.** \u2014 An advisor shall be compensated at a fixed rate that does not vary with whether a claimant files, whether a claim is allowed or denied, the amount allowed, whether the claimant accepts the scheduled amount or sues, or whether a matter is referred under subsection (a).\n\n**(e-2) Paid in addition.** \u2014 The cost of accreditation, of advisors, and of the tiers established by subsection (a) is paid in addition to amounts payable under \u00a7 12A-1, **and no part of it is charged against, deducted from, or funded out of amounts that would otherwise be paid to claimants.**\n\n**(f) An advisor may not be evaluated on referral rate.** No agency, program, or grantee may measure, rank, compensate, or evaluate an advisor by the proportion of claimants the advisor refers under subsection (a). **Referral rates shall be published by program and never by individual, and a claimant refused a referral may request one review of that refusal.**\n\n**(g) The advice is written.** The advisor shall furnish the claimant a brief written statement of the advice given, which satisfies \u00a7 12A-5(d).\n\n**(h) Volunteer protection.** A volunteer attorney or advisor is not liable for advice given in good faith under this Section.\n\n**(h-1) The attorney tier is funded, not merely permitted.** \u2014 The Attorney General shall compensate attorneys providing consultation under subsection (a) at a fixed rate under subsection (e-1).\n\n**(i) Onset.** \u2014 Each tier under subsection (a) shall be available not later than one year after the date of enactment. Where a tier is not available by that date, the Attorney General shall publish the fact, the reason, and the expected date of availability, and shall republish that statement every 90 days until the tier is available. **No right, deadline, or claim under this Section depends on a tier being available**, and the absence of a tier suspends nothing.\n\n**(i-1) And the statement says what would fix it.** \u2014 A statement published under subsection (i) shall state **the personnel and the amount required to make the tier available**, and shall be transmitted to the Congress with each republication."},{"anchor":"sec-12a-6a","heading":"\u00a7 12A-6A. Notice that this Section exists","part":"SECTION 12A \u2014 ADMINISTRATIVE COMPENSATION FOR SMALL VIOLATIONS","body":"**(a) With any response to a complaint.** \u2014 Where an agency responds to a complaint, grievance, or allegation concerning conduct that may be a violation of a right created by Sections 8 through 12, the response shall include a plain-language statement that this Section exists, what it compensates, that no fault need be shown, that filing is free and requires no lawyer, and how to file.\n\n**(b) Standing publication.** \u2014 Each agency shall publish that statement where it publishes information about complaints, and shall furnish it on request.\n\n**(c) No acknowledgment required.** \u2014 No agency may condition any action on a person signing or acknowledging the statement.\n\n**(d) Consequence of failure \u2014 self-executing.** \u2014 Where an agency has not furnished a statement required by subsection (a), **no period of limitation under this Section runs against that claimant with respect to that conduct** until the statement is furnished or the claimant acquires actual knowledge of this Section.\n\n**(e) Actual knowledge, and only actual knowledge.** \u2014 Knowledge under subsection (d) means the claimant in fact knew. **It is not established by what the claimant could have discovered, should have known, or was on notice to inquire into, by the publication of any statement under subsection (b), or by the passage of time.**\n\n**(f) Burden.** \u2014 The agency bears the burden of establishing knowledge under subsection (e)."},{"anchor":"sec-12a-7","heading":"\u00a7 12A-7. Publication","part":"SECTION 12A \u2014 ADMINISTRATIVE COMPENSATION FOR SMALL VIOLATIONS","body":"**(a)** The Attorney General shall publish annually, by agency, the number of claims filed, allowed, denied, and paid, and the total amounts paid.\n\n**(b) Counts, never individuals.** Publication under this Section is in aggregate form, and no claimant may be identified.\n\n**(c)** Publication is a ministerial duty."},{"anchor":"sec-12a-8","heading":"\u00a7 12A-8. States","part":"SECTION 12A \u2014 ADMINISTRATIVE COMPENSATION FOR SMALL VIOLATIONS","body":"**(a) The model.** The Attorney General shall publish, not later than one year after the date of enactment, model legislation establishing a system equivalent to this Section for the officers and employees of a State or unit of local government. **A State system satisfying subsection (c) qualifies whether or not the model has been published**, and the absence of the model delays no payment to such a State.\n\n**(b) Formula, not discretion.** A State that enacts the model without material modification receives a payment computed on population under the formula stated in the SCHEDULE. **The payment is made without application review, and no officer may condition, rank, prioritize, or delay it.**\n\n**(c) A State that designs its own \u2014 five features, and they are not negotiable.** A State system qualifies if it provides \u2014\n\n**(1)** a published schedule of amounts;\n\n**(2)** a decision deadline;\n\n**(3)** that failure to decide within the deadline allows the claim;\n\n**(4)** published counts of claims and amounts paid; and\n\n**(5)** that no claimant is required to be represented by counsel.\n\n**Everything else may vary. No other requirement may be imposed as a condition of the payment.**\n\n**(d) The undertaking.** A payment under this Section is conditioned on the State's undertaking to continue paying claims under its system after the payment is exhausted.\n\n**(e) States pay their own claims.** No payment under this Section may be used to pay a claim. **The United States funds the building of the system and does not assume liability for the conduct of a State or local officer.**\n\n**(f) Non-participation.** Where a State does not participate, this Section continues to apply to officers and employees of the United States within that State.\n\n**(g) Permanent appropriation.** There are appropriated such sums as are necessary for the payments under subsection (b) and under \u00a7 12A-8A(d), without fiscal year limitation. **A government that has qualified is paid without further appropriation**."},{"anchor":"sec-12a-8a","heading":"\u00a7 12A-8A. Tribal governments","part":"SECTION 12A \u2014 ADMINISTRATIVE COMPENSATION FOR SMALL VIOLATIONS","body":"**(a) Election.** The governing body of an Indian tribe included on the list published under section 104 of the Federally Recognized Indian Tribe List Act of 1994 (25 U.S.C. \u00a7 5131) may elect to establish a system equivalent to this Section for the officers and employees of the tribe. **The election is the tribe's alone.** Nothing in this Act requires it, conditions any other Federal payment or program on it, or attaches any consequence to its absence.\n\n**(b) The model, adapted in consultation.** The Attorney General shall publish, with the model legislation under \u00a7 12A-8(a), a version adapted for enactment as tribal law, prepared in consultation with Tribal governments. **A tribal system satisfying subsection (c) qualifies whether or not the adapted model has been published**, and the absence of the model delays no payment.\n\n**(c) Qualification.** A tribal system qualifies if it provides the five features stated in \u00a7 12A-8(c). **Everything else may vary \u2014 including the forum, which is the tribe's own** \u2014 and no other requirement may be imposed as a condition of the payment.\n\n**(d) Formula, not discretion.** An electing tribe whose system qualifies receives a payment computed on enrolled population under the formula stated in the SCHEDULE. **The payment is made without application review, and no officer may condition, rank, prioritize, or delay it.**\n\n**(e) The undertaking, and its limits.** A payment under this Section is conditioned on the tribe's undertaking to continue paying claims under its system after the payment is exhausted. The undertaking includes the tribe's consent to claims **under its own system, in its own forum, and on its own system's terms \u2014 and to nothing else.** No election under this Section waives the tribe's immunity from suit in any court of the United States or of any State, subjects the tribe or its officers to this Act, or makes any officer or employee of the tribe a covered defendant under \u00a7 0-3(a).\n\n**(f) Tribes pay their own claims.** No payment under this Section may be used to pay a claim. **The United States funds the building of the system and does not assume liability for the conduct of a tribal officer.**\n\n**(g) Non-election.** Where a tribe does not elect, nothing changes: this Act does not reach conduct under color of tribal law, the Indian Civil Rights Act and tribal forums continue to govern it, **and the omission is a determination of Congress and not a gap.**"},{"anchor":"sec-13-1","heading":"\u00a7 13-1. Effective date; application","part":"SECTION 13 \u2014 GENERAL PROVISIONS","body":"This Act takes effect on enactment and applies to conduct occurring on or after that date. **No provision revives a claim barred before enactment.**","partHeads":[{"heading":"SECTION 13 \u2014 GENERAL PROVISIONS","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-13-2","heading":"\u00a7 13-2. Rights created by this Act; rule of construction","part":"SECTION 13 \u2014 GENERAL PROVISIONS","body":"**(a) General rule.** Except as provided in subsection (b), this Act creates no right; it provides remedies for the deprivation of rights that exist independently of it.\n\n**(b) Rights created.** Each Section designated in subsection (e) states a standard of conduct and creates a right, enforceable under this Act, not to be subjected to conduct departing from that standard. Congress creates these rights in the exercise of its **ordinary power to create statutory rights**, as it has done in other Acts securing individual rights against public and private conduct. A right created by this Act is independent of any right secured by the Constitution, and **neither its existence nor its content depends upon the construction given by any court to any constitutional provision addressing similar conduct.**\n\n**(c) Source of power; classes of defendant.** Subsection (b) applies \u2014\n1. to a covered defendant acting under color of Federal law or authority, as an exercise of the power of Congress to **prescribe the conduct of officers and employees of the United States** and to provide remedies for persons injured by it; and\n2. to a covered defendant acting under color of State law, as an exercise of the power of Congress under **section 5 of the Fourteenth Amendment** and upon the findings in \u00a7 0-2(6).\n\n**(d) Severability by class and by Section.**\n1. If subsection (c)(2) is held invalid, in whole or as to any designated Section, **subsection (c)(1) and every other provision of this Act continue in effect**, and each right created by subsection (b) continues to be enforceable against every covered defendant acting under color of Federal law or authority.\n2. If subsection (b) is held invalid in its entirety, **Sections 1 through 7 continue in effect** as remedies for rights existing independently of this Act, and **no amendment made by this Act to any other statute is affected.**\n3. The invalidity of the designation of any Section under subsection (e) does not affect the designation of any other.\n\n**(e) Designated Sections.** Section 6-1 (retaliation); **Section 8** (conditions of confinement); **Section 9** (use of force); **Section 10** (observing and recording official conduct); **Section 11** (identification); **Section 12** (consent).\n\n**(f) Preserved.** Nothing in subsection (b) enlarges the abrogation effected by \u00a7 3-2, which reaches only claims arising from deprivations of rights secured by the Fourteenth Amendment. A right created by this Act is enforceable against a State only to the extent a State is otherwise subject to suit."},{"anchor":"sec-13-3","heading":"\u00a7 13-3. Severability","part":"SECTION 13 \u2014 GENERAL PROVISIONS","body":"Each Section, and each subsection of each Section, is severable. **Congress would have enacted \u00a7 1-1 independently of every other provision of this Act, and would have enacted Sections 1, 2, and 3 independently of one another.** The invalidity of any provision as applied to any class of defendant does not affect its application to any other class."},{"anchor":"sec-13-4","heading":"\u00a7 13-4. Resources and continuity","part":"SECTION 13 \u2014 GENERAL PROVISIONS","body":"No provision of this Act depends upon the existence, funding, or staffing of any office, board, or commission. **No consequence provided in this Act may be suspended, waived, or declined to be enforced by any officer of the United States.**"},{"anchor":"sec-13-5","heading":"\u00a7 13-5. Application without regard to citizenship or status","part":"SECTION 13 \u2014 GENERAL PROVISIONS","body":"No provision of this Act, and no right, remedy, defense, or procedure it provides, applies differently by reason of a person's **citizenship, nationality, immigration status, or the lawfulness of the person's presence.** Congress provides the protections of this Act to **every person**, and the omission of any such distinction from this Act is a determination by Congress that none is to be drawn."},{"anchor":"schedule","heading":"SCHEDULE","part":null,"body":"1. **Intake health screening** (\u00a7 8-3A(b)(1)): [___ hours of arrival]\n2. **Response to a request for care** (\u00a7 8-3A(b)(2)): [___ hours; immediate where the request describes an emergent condition]\n3. **Referral to a specialist ordered under \u00a7 8-3A(b)(4)** (\u00a7 8-3A(b)(4)): [___ days]\n4. **Preservation period after a death or serious injury in custody** (\u00a7 8-3C(c)): [___ years]\n5. **Share of periods missed at a facility that constitutes a pattern** (\u00a7 8-3A(h-1)): [___ percent in a year]\n6. **Period after which an ordered referral is authorized by operation of law** (\u00a7 8-3A(i)): [___ days after the period in item 3 has run]\n7. **Custodian size above which the publication duty applies** (\u00a7 8-3A(h-2)): [___ persons held on average]\n5. **Categories of violation for which no fault need be shown, and the presumptive amount for each** (\u00a7 12A-1(c)): [reserved for the drafting committee \u2014 one row per category of violation of the rights created by Sections 8 through 12, each with a presumptive amount]\n6. **Period after which an individualized condition of confinement requires a recorded objective** (\u00a7 8-2A(d-1)): [___ days]\n7. **Period for the agency to allow or deny a small claim** (\u00a7 12A-2(b)): [___ days]\n8. **Period to seek reconsideration of a denial** (\u00a7 12A-2(f)): [___ days]\n9. **Detention period constituting a scheduled harm** (\u00a7 12A-5(a)(4)): [___ hours]\n10. **Amount above which a written statement of what is waived is required before a waiver is effective** (\u00a7 12A-5(d)): [$___]\n11. **State small-claims payment formula, computed on population** (\u00a7 12A-8(b)): [reserved for the drafting committee]\n12. **Tribal small-claims payment formula, computed on enrolled population** (\u00a7 12A-8A(d)): [reserved for the drafting committee]\n13. **Period after a clinician records that needed care cannot be furnished where the person is held** (\u00a7 8-3E(b)(1)): [___ days]\n14. **Period after a \u00a7 8-3A(i) referral is authorized and not furnished** (\u00a7 8-3E(b)(2)): [___ days]\n15. **Interruption of a prescribed medication that establishes a lack of capacity** (\u00a7 8-3E(b)(3)): [___ hours]\n16. **Period to furnish the care, transfer, or apply for release** (\u00a7 8-3E(c)): [___ days]\n17. **Period for a State court to decide an application before State process is ineffective** (\u00a7 8-3E(e)): [___ days]\n18. **Period after an unmet request for care that establishes a lack of capacity** (\u00a7 8-3E(b)(4)): [___ days]"}],"asOf":"2026-10-14"}}}};
